{"id": "181843", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=181843", "title": "Byndoor", "text": "Byndoor is the northernmost taluka of Udupi district, spanning from the coast to the foothills of the western ghats. The Kollur Mookambika temple is located around 28 km from the town of Byndoor.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nMangalore International Airport (), at Bajpe, about 20 km from the city centre. There are daily flights to Mumbai, Bangalore, Goa, Kochi and Calicut in the domestic segment and weekly or bi-weekly flights to Dubai, Abu Dhabi, Muscat (Oman), Doha (Qatar), Kuwait and Bahrain in the international segment. Byndoor is two hours drive from Mangalore.\nBy train.\nByndoor (Mookambika Road Station - BYNR) is the nearest station. To get to Kollur, from the railway station catch a rickshaw to Byndoor, and from Byndoor to Kollur private bus facility is available but at limited intervals. Other option is private taxi from Byndoor town or from the railway station. Big disadvantage of Byndoor station is that it is located in a remote border area of Kundapura taluka. \nKundapura (KUDA) is the best railway station to reach Kollur because you can get more buses from here. But the Kundapura Railway Station is 10 km from the city and autorickshaws charge about \u20b9120 to the city bus stand. Kollur is about 38 km from the railway station. \nThe station has good waiting room/hall and a fruit stall. From Kundapura there are plenty of private express buses are available (every 15 min) to Kollur. Plenty of private taxis are also available. Nearest route to Kollur from the station is via Basrur-Gulvady Vended Dam Bridge (Kundapura Station-Basrur-Gulvadi Vented Dam Bridge-Neralkatte-Vandse-Kollur), its lesser than 35km journey. Railway Station telephone number: +91-8254-237365.\nGet around.\nAutorickshaws are available everywhere and they charge \u20b925/- for shorter distances.\nDo.\nTrekking possibilities are numerous. You can start from the bridge at Kashi Theertha river spot 1 km from the temple gate. Auto fare. \u20b925.\nSleep.\nThere are a number of lodges around the Mookambika temple. Private hotels are mostly situated between the busstand and the temple gate and some of them offer rooms at \u20b9500-2,000 per night. \nThere are two lodges inside the temple gate. The first one is called Hotel Shwetha. \nThe second one is a guest house run by the temple administration. Rates: \u20b9500 for a double room with bath. The rooms are new, spacious and attached with clean bathrooms. \nSeveral cheaper lodges are also available behind the parking space on the Shimoga road. They offer rooms at \u20b9200 per night and dormitories at lower prices."}
{"id": "181847", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=181847", "title": "We\u00dfling", "text": "We\u00dfling is in the \"Landkreis\" Starnberg Upper Bavaria. While the town name itself may not ring a bell, one of its parts, Oberpfaffenhofen, is famous for its aerospace and astronomy facilities."}
{"id": "181851", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=181851", "title": "Harlowton", "text": "Harlowton is in central Montana. About 950 people live there. It is the county seat of Wheatland County.\nUnderstand.\nHarlowton is surrounded by the Crazy, Little Belt and Big Snowy Mountains, which are a part of the Lewis and Clark National Forest. \nGet in.\nHarlowton is at the intersection of U.S. Highway 12 (US-12), U.S. Highway 191 (US-191) and State Highway 3 (MT-3). It is about an hour-and-a-half northwest of Billings and 2 hours east of Helena.\nGet around.\nAs a small town, there is no public transportation. Everything in town is within walking distance.\nWhen adventuring outside of town, be sure you plan for fuel and download maps. Wide open country is great to explore, just don't expect to always have service and amenities.\nDo.\nThere are three parks located within Harlowton: Chief Joseph Park, Deer Park, and Fischer Park.\nDrink.\nThe 3 bars are within a 2 block area."}
{"id": "181858", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=181858", "title": "Townsend (Montana)", "text": "Townsend is a town in Montana. It is the county seat of Broadwater County and about 1,700 people live there. Townsend is at the southern tip of Canyon Ferry Reservoir, a year-round popular spot for boaters and fisherman.\nGet in.\nTownsend is at the intersection of US-12 and US-287, 34 miles southeast of Helena.\nFrom Helena, take exit 192 from Interstate 15 in Helena or exit 274 from Interstate 90 near Three Forks.\nGet around.\nAs a small town, there is no public transportation. It is easy to walk to any location.\nDo.\nThe area around Townsend is popular for hunting, and also offers opportunities to hike, camp, ATV, and snowmobile."}
{"id": "181867", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=181867", "title": "White Sulphur Springs (Montana)", "text": "White Sulphur Springs is in the Smith River Valley of Central Montana, and is the county seat of Meagher County. It is known for its namesake hot springs.\nGet in.\nWhite Sulphur Springs is at the intersection of U.S. Highway 12 (US-12) and U.S. Highway 89 (US-89), about an hour-and-a-half east of Helena.\nGet around.\nAs a small town, there is no public transportation. When adventuring outside of town, be sure you plan for fuel and download maps. Wide open country is great to explore, just don't expect to always have service and amenities."}
