{"id": "182287", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182287", "title": "Maltah\u00f6he", "text": "Maltah\u00f6he is a village of 3,500 people (2023) in the Hardap region of central Namibia.\nUnderstand.\nMaltah\u00f6he was named after \"Malta von Burgsdorff\", wife of a German imperial officer. It is one of the historic colonial places in Namibia. There is not much to see here, but its location on several regional roads make it a rather frequently visited place.\nGet in.\nMaltah\u00f6he is connected via tarred road (C19) to Mariental, and via good gravel roads to Walvis Bay and Helmeringhausen (C14), to Solitaire (MR36), and to Kalkrand (C21).\nGet around.\nThe village is walkable.\nBuy.\nMaltah\u00f6he has a petrol station and a general dealer.\nEat.\nThe general dealer on the main road offers snacks and light meals.\nConnect.\nMaltah\u00f6he is connected to the mobile network, both from MTC[dead link] and Telecom[dead link]."}
{"id": "182288", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182288", "title": "Lagos City/Victoria Island", "text": "Victoria Island is an affluent area that encompasses a former island of the same name, neighbouring Lagos Island, Ikoyi and the end of the Lekki Peninsula by the Lagos Lagoon. It is the main business and financial centre of Lagos in Lagos State. Victoria Island is one of the most exclusive and expensive areas to reside in Lagos.\nUnderstand.\nPrior to British colonisation, Victoria Island was surrounded entirely by water. It was bordered by the Atlantic Ocean on the south, the mouth of the Lagos Lagoon on the west, the Five Cowrie Creek to the north, and swamps on the east. The British then began filling in the eastern swamps to reduce mosquito breeding areas, creating a land bridge between Victoria Island and Lekki Peninsula, putting and end to its existence as a true island.\nAfter independence, successive state governments expanded this development, culminating in the construction of a highway connecting Victoria Island to Epe. This activity, along with the rapid commercialisation of Victoria Island, served to stimulate residential development along the Lekki-Epe corridor, starting with Lekki Phase 1. The area of the land bridge, composed of the former swampland, became a large slum called Maroko Town which housed many of the new migrants to Lagos State. Residents of the island complained about this problem, leading the military Governor of the State, Raji Rasaki, to forcibly remove the residents on 14 July 1990, resulting in numerous injuries. Governor Rasaki and his armed security forces caused the eviction of as many as 300,000 residents, some of whom had legal title to their property.\nThis new area established after the evictions was called Victoria Island Annex. It was cleared and sold to residential buyers. Subsequent reclamation expanded the area to the extent that Victoria Island Annex is now connected to the Lekki Peninsula. This new, enlarged area is referred to as \"Oniru Estate\" after the ruling family of the area.\nIt was designated as an upscale residential area, but failing infrastructure and overcrowding in the old business district on Lagos Island, and lax zoning enforcement in Victoria Island led to a mass migration of businesses. Today, Victoria Island is one of Nigeria's busiest centres of banking and commerce, with most major Nigerian and international corporations headquartered on the island.\nThe island has continued to rapidly develop, and, along with Ikoyi, is a favourite spot for both Nigerians and foreigners to live and play. However the influx of banks and other commercial ventures has changed the formerly serene atmosphere of the island. \nA new project being developed by the Chagoury Group includes the Eko Atlantic City, next to Victoria Island. The project is being built on reclaimed land that has been lost to coastal erosion.\nClimate.\nVictoria Island in Lagos experiences a tropical savanna (Aw) climate, with consistently hot and humid conditions throughout the year. The weather is defined by clear wet and dry seasons, average daytime temperatures of about 30\u00b0C, very high humidity levels ranging from roughly 80% to 100%, and heavy rainfall, particularly during the rainy months. This climate pattern is influenced by its location close to the equator.\nWet Season is between April/May to October, with heavy downpours.Dry Season is from the Month of November to March/April, generally less rainy but still humid. \nCope.\nVictoria Island hosts most of the diplomatic presence in Lagos, many of which were the embassies to Nigeria prior to the move of the capital to Abuja. The now consulates, embassy branch offices, or Deputy High Commissions on Victoria Island include Benin, Brazil, Canada, China, Germany, India, Indonesia, Italy, Lebanon, the Netherlands, Russia, South Africa, the United Kingdom, and the United States."}
{"id": "182294", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182294", "title": "Valkeakoski", "text": "Valkeakoski is a town in Finland, about 40\u00a0km from Tampere to southeast.\nUnderstand.\nValkeakoski is a traditional industrial town and its history is strongly linked to the development of factories and industry. Today, the town still has many industrial enterprises, such as a paper mill and a machine shop. New sectors such as information technology and the service sector have also sought to diversify the city's economy.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nDirect coaches from Helsinki and H\u00e4meenlinna. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto.\nBy train.\nToijala is the nearest railway station. There is a bus between Toijala and Valkeakoski which goes few times per day at weekdays, see timetables[dead link].\nGet around.\nBy bus.\n\"See Tampere#Get around for information about fares and tickets\"\nValkeakoski has a local bus system, which works mainly at daytime on weekdays. At the other times, it is possible to use longer-distance routes. The Nysse route planner[dead link] is useful for getting around by bus in Valkeakoski.\nSampobussi offers flexible service in the town and surrounding villages. The downtown stop is at market square (Kauppatori) by the fountain.\nBy scooter.\nYou can rent electric kick scooters for use in the centre from Finnish Swup. There are two Kontio Kruiser electric scooters in Hotel Waltikka.\nBy bike.\nBicycles can be rented from Apianlahti campsite, Hotel Waltikka, Ilola Rural Tourism and Voipaala Manor Caf\u00e9."}
{"id": "182296", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182296", "title": "Urjala", "text": "Urjala is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nNearby Toijala and Viiala in Akaa have railway stations. Timetables are available from VR website.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses from Tampere and Turku. During the school year, some services also from Huittinen, Akaa, Valkeakoski and Sastamala. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto."}
{"id": "182297", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182297", "title": "Ruovesi", "text": "Ruovesi is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland, with 200 lakes (18% of the area) and quite some forest.\nGet in.\nRoad 66 from Orivesi leads via Ruovesi and Virrat to Lapua.\nIf you come by train, bus or bike and stay the night in Tampere or Virrat, coming the last leg by heritage lake steamer would be a nice alternative. Services twice weekly from each.\nIf coming by coach or train, check the last leg, as bus services are sparse.\nBy train.\nNearby Vilppula and Juupajoki have railway stations, serviced by regional trains from Tampere, Orivesi or Keuruu, (Haapam\u00e4ki).\nBy bus.\nCoaches from Tampere (1\u00bd hr, often transfer in Orivesi) and from Kokkola (4 hr). For timetables, see Matkahuolto. Osmo Aho's route \"Pohjanmaan pikavuoro\" between Tampere and Kokkola stops at Ruovesi; its tickets are sold at the Onnibus web site. There are also local buses from Tampere and M\u00e4ntt\u00e4-Vilppula. Coming from other directions, try to guess possible transfer options, deducing from the last leg bus.\nBy boat.\nRuovesi is on the 130-km Toisvesi\u2013Ruovesi\u2013N\u00e4sij\u00e4rvi waterway. From Virrat centre max draught is 2.4 m, max height is 7 m (?). N\u00e4sij\u00e4rvi's outlet is the rapids through Tampere.\nEat.\nSeveral grill or fast food places. Few real restaurants, except catering services. Some lodgings serve meals."}
{"id": "182298", "revid": "1806", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182298", "title": "P\u00e4lk\u00e4ne", "text": "P\u00e4lk\u00e4ne is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses from Tampere and Lahti. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto."}
{"id": "182299", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182299", "title": "Punkalaidun", "text": "Punkalaidun is a municipality of 2,800 people (2020) in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nUnderstand.\nPunkalaitumenjoki (Punkalaidun River) flows through the municipality, along which most villages, including the church village, are located.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nNearby Humppila has a railway station. Timetables are available from VR website.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses from Tampere, Sastamala and Huittinen. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto.\nBy car.\nTaikay\u00f6ntie (regional roads 230 and 232) runs in the area of \u200b\u200bPunkalaidun, Huittinen and Urjala. \nHighway 2 between Helsinki and Pori goes through the municipality.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tampere-Pirkkala Airport.\nDo.\nTaikay\u00f6ntie (regional roads 230 and 232) is a historically valuable tourist route and has great natural and river landscapes, as well as a cultural environment. "}
{"id": "182300", "revid": "2244977", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182300", "title": "Kihni\u00f6", "text": "Kihni\u00f6 is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nGet in.\nThe municipal centre is just north of Highway 23 leading from Parkano to Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4. \nBy train.\nNearby Parkano has a railway station. Timetables are available from VR website.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses from Parkano. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto.\nGet around.\nCall-a-ride.\nCall-a-ride[dead link] is service traffic open to everyone within Kihni\u00f6. Order for the ride must be made no later than the previous day by calling the motorist driving the route in question. There is a charge for the ride based on the bus fare. The route to be taken depends on who is coming on board. For more information, e.g. schedules for each route can be obtained from the taxi operator operating it."}
{"id": "182301", "revid": "2244977", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182301", "title": "Juupajoki", "text": "Juupajoki is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality is mainly rural and forest, but there is also an old factory environment and several bogs. The centre of the municipality, Korkeakoski, was born in connection with the establishment of a shoe factory. \nGet in.\nJuupajoki is about 20 km north-west of Orivesi along M\u00e4nt\u00e4ntie (from M\u00e4ntt\u00e4). \nBy train.\nJuupajoki has a. Timetables are available from the VR website. You can get to Juupajoki from Tampere or Orivesi by train.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance depart buses from Tampere. For timetables, see Matkahuolto.\nGet around.\nKorkeakoski can easily be explored by foot. Because the municipality is large, the easiest way to get around elsewhere is by car.\nDo.\nMost of Juupajoki is covered by forest, and you should head out to pick berries. You get cloudberries in the swamps, and elsewhere there is a wealth of lingonberries.\nYou can also visit the area to fish. In the river you can find e.g. brown trout, and in the countryside there is pike and perch. For reel-and-rod fishing you need to pay the national fishing fee (if aged 18\u201365). Without reel, the right to access may suffice. For the river you probably need a day card regardless. One rod per person.\nBuy.\nCrafts from the Kallenautio tavern.\nBigL leather clothes or honey of Juupajoki from the factory store of the Koskenjalka Museum.\nDrink.\nDon't come here for nightlife. For that, head to Orivesi, M\u00e4ntt\u00e4, Tampere or Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4.\nSleep.\nThe camping ground is on the northern border of the municipality, next to the ski centre.\nYou might be able to find some agritourism farms.\nStay safe.\nJuupajoki has a health centre, but in more serious cases you should go to M\u00e4ntt\u00e4, Orivesi, Ruovesi or Tampere Central Hospital.\nGo next.\nEn route to Ruovesi you pass by Ry\u00f6v\u00e4rinkuoppa (\"robber's hole\") and Helvetinkolu (\"hell gorge\"). Bon voyage!"}
{"id": "182302", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182302", "title": "H\u00e4meenkyr\u00f6", "text": "H\u00e4meenkyr\u00f6 is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nNearby Nokia and Tampere have railway stations. Timetables are available from the VR website.\nBy bus.\nCoaches from Tampere, Sein\u00e4joki, Kankaanp\u00e4\u00e4 and Sastamala. For timetables, see Matkahuolto."}
{"id": "182303", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182303", "title": "Virrat", "text": "Virrat is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality is rich in lakes, which is why Virrat is well known for water sports and fishing.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nNeighboring Parkano and Haapam\u00e4ki have railway stations. Timetables are available from VR website.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses from Tampere and Parkano. For timetables of ordinary coaches, see Matkahuolto. Osmo Aho route \"Pohjanmaan pikavuoro\" between Tampere and Kokkola stops at Virrat, its tickets are sold at Onnibus web site.\nBy boat.\nVirrat is on the 130-km N\u00e4sij\u00e4rvi\u2013Toisvesi waterway. Between N\u00e4sij\u00e4rvi in Tampere and Virrat centre, the route leads from one scenic labyrinthine lake to the next (N\u00e4sij\u00e4rvi, Palovesi, Ruovesi, Tarjanne and Vaskivesi), all of them with many islands; max draught is 2.4 m. The route connects to a 1.0/1.9-m fairway from M\u00e4ntt\u00e4-Vilppula. On Toisvesi, reachable through the centre, max draught is 1.8 m."}
{"id": "182304", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182304", "title": "Parkano", "text": "Parkano is a town in Pirkanmaa, Finland. In 2021, it had 6,300 residents. \nParkano has various places to visit, nature experiences and events\nUnderstand.\nParkano is a traditional industrial town with a history strongly linked to wood processing and the paper industry. Today, the town's industry has diversified, with metal and electronics industries, among others, operating in the area.\nGet in.\nParkano is a traffic hub, lying on national roads 3 (E12) and 23, on regional roads 274 and 332, and on the Finnish main railway line. Road 3 leads from Helsinki via Tampere and Parkano to Vaasa, where E12 merges with the Blue Highway and continues to Mo i Rana in Norway. Road 23 leads from Pori via Parkano, Keuruu and Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 to Joensuu. Road 274 comes from Karvia and road 332 from Kuru.\nBy train.\nParkano is on the main Finnish railway, between Helsinki and Oulu, at the section Tampere\u2013Sein\u00e4joki. However, the railway station in the centre is on the defunct Pori\u2013Haapam\u00e4ki line. There is no connection bus for the 7 km from the station on the main line, but buses between Parkano and Kihni\u00f6 can be used if they happen to combine. Timetables for the trains are available from VR.\nBy bus.\nThere are coaches from Helsinki, Tampere, Sein\u00e4joki, Vaasa, Kankaanp\u00e4\u00e4 and Keuruu, the last two on school days only. They stop at the centre. For timetables, see Matkahuolto."}
{"id": "182305", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182305", "title": "Pirkkala", "text": "Pirkkala is a municipality in Pirkanmaa, Finland, adjacent to Tampere.\nUnderstand.\nThe former parish of Suur-Pirkkala (Great Pirkkala) is mentioned in historical writings from the 14th century. Originally, it occupied over half of the present-day region of Pirkanmaa (named after Pirkkala). Suur-Pirkkala began to split when new parishes and municipalities were founded \u2013 including Tampere.\nGet in.\nNational road 3 (E12), here acting as Tampere western bypass, goes through Pirkkala.\nBy plane.\n () is located in Pirkkala, 17\u00a0km southwest of the city of Tampere. The airport offers a few destinations.\nBy train.\nNearby Tampere has a railway station. Timetables are available from VR website.\nGet around.\n\"See Tampere#Get around for information about bus tickets and fares\""}
{"id": "182319", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182319", "title": "Suisun City", "text": "Suisun City is in Solano County, in the North Bay region of California.\nGet around.\nThe easiest way to get around in Suisun City is by car."}
{"id": "182324", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182324", "title": "Val Marie", "text": "Val Marie is a tiny village in Southwestern Saskatchewan and is considered the gateway to Grasslands National Park.\nUnderstand.\nVal Marie, population 120 (2021), is the gateway to Grasslands National Park. The village is adjacent to the West Block, and hosts most of the local Parks Canada bureaucracy. Without this major institutional employer, the community would most likely be another one of Saskatchewan's many ghost towns.\nSaskatchewan residents often fail to appreciate their native prairie environment, and the locals are more likely to take their holidays at nearby Lac Pelletier or Saskatchewan Landing. If you've experienced the typical summer overcrowding at Banff or the Cypress Hills, you may consider this a good thing. Val Marie is a relatively niche destination without much family RV traffic.\nVal Marie has a few services: a self-service gas station, a hotel/bar, a museum (which also includes a gift shop, coffee shop and art gallery), a campground, art galleries, bed and breakfasts, an old convent that has been converted to an inn, a movie theatre, a hockey arena and curling rink, a library, a community centre, a town square and a general store. Grasslands is considered a seasonal attraction and most tourist services shut down for the long Canadian winter.\nThe nearest full-service centre is the city of Swift Current, located just over an hour to the north.\nHistory.\nThe community of Val Marie \u2013 Valley of Mary \u2013 was founded in 1910 by Father Passaplan, Louis Denniel, and the brothers Fran\u00e7ois and L\u00e9on Pinel. Most of the early settlers were ranchers and farmers from Quebec and France. Val Marie incorporated as a village on September 13, 1926. By 1939, two irrigation reservoirs were built near Val Marie by the PFRA (Prairie Farm Rehabilitation Agency) in coordination with Ducks Unlimited. Over 10,000 acres (40 km\u00b2) are irrigated through the system which provides some stabilization through droughts which plague the area.\nOriginally settled by French ranchers, the village remained largely ethnically French and by the 1950s had 450 people. Amenities then included three gasoline stations, a hospital, convent, bakery, bank and several cafes and stores. The population declined significantly with low grain commodity prices and droughts in the 1980s. English is now universally spoken as the last unilingual Francophone resident died in 1981.\nGet in.\nVal Marie is located at the intersection of Highways 4 and 18, about 120 km south of Swift Current and 360 km southwest of Regina. It is about 30 km from the Morgan-Monchy Border Crossing from Montana, USA, and about 120 km north of Malta, which has a train station on the Empire Builder Amtrak train.\nGet around.\nThe village is very small and is easily walkable; however, you will need a private car to get there.\nDo.\nWalk around town. This tiny community is full of modestly interesting historical markers.\nCheck out rush hour, 4:30pm - 5pm. The Park's seasonal employees almost all have walking commutes, and can be spotted by their green uniforms.\nWave to everyone and every vehicle you see on the street!\nConnect.\nVal Marie's tiny library is only open two days a week, but its free wi-fi is available 24/7. Locals can sometimes be spotted parked next to the building for exactly this purpose."}
{"id": "182354", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182354", "title": "Ruisui", "text": "Ruisui (\u745e\u7a57\u9109) is a rural township located in southern Hualien County, famous for its Hot Springs and the Siouguluan River Basin.\nUnderstand.\nThe population is composed of Hoklo, Hakka, and Taiwanese aborigines, most of whom are Amis. Agriculture and tourism are major industries."}
{"id": "182355", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182355", "title": "Budapest/V\u00edziv\u00e1ros", "text": "V\u00edziv\u00e1ros \"(lit. Water Town)\" is in Budapest. This article describes the outer part of the 1st district[dead link], which includes parts of Gell\u00e9rthegy (lit. \"Gell\u00e9rt Hill\"), Krisztinav\u00e1ros (lit. \"Kristina Town\"), Tab\u00e1n and V\u00edziv\u00e1ros. However, this article doesn't include the northern part of the V\u00edziv\u00e1ros, which is part of the 2nd district and is described in North Buda.\nUnderstand.\nIn Roman times, the north-south transport route parallel to the Danube to Aquincum led through the area. The settlement of the Romans here was destroyed by the Sarmatians in the 3rd century. In the Middle Ages, several small settlements were established in this area, each named after its church. In the time of King Sigismund, their inhabitants fortified these settlements with a town wall. From the Middle Ages, there was also a port here, where a lively commercial life took place. After the occupation of Buda, the Turks converted the temples into mosques. In 1686, during the fighting for the recapture of Buda, the area was almost completely destroyed. After the siege, German merchants and craftsmen were settled here, who built their houses and churches in Baroque style. At the end of the 19th century, the banks of the Danube were lined with rows of palaces, which is part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site. Some of the small streets, monumental churches and residential houses of the district still retain the atmosphere of old times.\nGet in.\n\u00a0M2\u00a0 has three metro stations located at the north side of the district:, and.\nTwo major tram lines crosses the district:\nMajor bus lines that come from the Pest side of Danube:\nSee.\nV\u00edziv\u00e1ros.\nAround Adam Clark Square (Clark \u00c1d\u00e1m t\u00e9r).\nAll area of these district what is east from Castle Hill.\nAround Batthy\u00e1ny Square (Batthy\u00e1ny t\u00e9r).\nAll area of these district what is north from Castle Hill.\nKrisztinav\u00e1ros.\nAll area of these district what is west from Castle Hill.\nTab\u00e1n.\nAll area of these district what is south from Castle Hill.\nEat.\nBudget.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nMid-range.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nSplurge.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn Tab\u00e1n:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nDrink.\nCafe and breakfast.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nCafe and confectionery.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn Tab\u00e1n:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nPubs.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn Tab\u00e1n:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:\nBars.\nIn Krisztinav\u00e1ros:\nIn V\u00edziv\u00e1ros:"}
{"id": "182364", "revid": "2296660", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182364", "title": "Dongfang", "text": "Dongfang (\u4e1c\u65b9, D\u014dngf\u0101ng) is a city on the west coast of Hainan Island in southern China.\nUnderstand.\nDongfang is a county-level city with jurisdiction over 8 towns and 2 townships. The largest urban center is the coastal town of Basuo, which is home to about 40% of the population.\nGet around.\nA public bus service operates in Basuo. The fare is usually one yuan for short trips within the urban area. Taxis are also available.\nThe Dongfang Bus Station in Basuo has bus services to all the smaller towns in Dongfang, but places in rural Dongfang are generally best reached by car.\nSleep.\nMost of the hotels in Dongfang that accept foreigners are situated in Basuo. The Jerome Hotel &amp; Resort at Jinyue Bay is an exception."}
{"id": "182367", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182367", "title": "Lourinh\u00e3", "text": "Lourinh\u00e3 is a town and municipality of 26,000 people (2011) in the Oeste region of Portugal. It has extensive, picturesque beaches and, more recently, by the paleontological remains, which include fossilised bones, footprints, eggs and embryos from Jurassic dinosaurs, many of which can be seen at the local museum, Museu da Lourinh\u00e3. Lourinh\u00e3 is one of the few brandy-making areas, besides Cognac, Armagnac and Jerez, to have received appellation status.\nUnderstand.\nThe economy of Lourinh\u00e3 (loh-ree-NYUHN, /\u026bo.\u027ei.\u02c8\u0272\u0250\u0303/) is mainly based on fishing, agriculture, the construction industry, forestry and manufacturing. About 80% of its territory is used in agriculture, with potatoes, Rocha pear, and vineyards, among others.\nClimate.\nLourinh\u00e3 has an oceanic climate due to its proximity to the Atlantic Ocean. Temperatures can vary in summer from 24 C to 30 C and the nights in the municipality are cool. In winter, negative temperatures are very rare, but can go down to -4 C. Lourinh\u00e3 is known for its rainfall and is frequently affected by floods in autumn.\nGet in.\nLourinh\u00e3 is 63 km north of Lisbon on the A8 road.\nBuy.\nThe Lourinh\u00e3 Municipal Market is in the centre of the village, next to the town hall. Fruits, vegetables, fish, meat, dresses and alcohol can be found here. There are also three butchers, cafes and barbecues on the ground floor, and a restaurant on the first floor.\nLourinh\u00e3 is a wine-producing municipality: spirits of recognized quality have been produced since the middle of the 20th century. The Controlled Denomination of Origin \"Lourinh\u00e3\" for brandy is the only one of its kind in Portugal and the third in Europe, after the famous Cognac and Armagnac."}
{"id": "182368", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182368", "title": "Albinen", "text": "Albinen is in Valais.\nUnderstand.\nAlbinen is a small mountain village that overlooks the Rh\u00f4ne valley. It is ranked among the most beautiful villages in Switzerland[dead link] and exudes olde-worlde charm thanks to an intact village centre with wooden chalets, some of which are over 500 years old. The village preserves the tradition Valais building styles.\nGet in.\nAlbinen can be reached by minibus from Leuk Bahnhof station in the Rh\u00f4ne valley, where fast trains from Brig and Geneva stop. Since the road is quite narrow, only small minibuses serve the route and there are only 8 services per day. A separate route connects Albinen with Leukerbad and serves as the ski shuttle in winter. [dead link]\nGet around.\nAlbinen is a small village of around 500 people, so you can easily walk around on foot. In fact the centre of the village is completely inaccessible by car because the cobbled streets are extremely narrow and the wooden chalets are packed very close together.\nSee.\nThe easiest way to discover Albinen is to follow the \u201cEgguweg\u201d, a walking tour with numerous information posts that explain the different facets of the village, from dry stone walls to farming and medicinal herb gardens. A stroll through the village centre is a must, taking in the sun-scorched wooden buildings and the narrow alleyways, as well as the incongruous church that dominates the village and is the most modern construction in the village centre, having been built in 1954 after the previous church was destroyed in an earthquake.\nDo.\nAlbinen is a hiker\u2019s paradise, with trails leading to Leukerbad (via the Albinenleitern - a series of wooden ladders attached to the cliff face on the hike up from Leukerbad), the Torrentalp and across to the equally scenic villages of Guttet-Feschel.\nBudding potters can try their hand at traditional pottery making at the Topferhuus, which offers workshops.\nBuy.\nWith only one shop in the village, offering all the essentials for the local population, there is little opportunity for shopping in Albinen. \nEat.\nThere are only two restaurants in the village centre, one of which - the Godswargjistubu - only opens on reservation. The other is the Sunnublick, which has a small terrace overlooking the Rh\u00f4ne valley and offers typical Valais dishes such as r\u00f6sti and fondue.\nDrink.\nOther than the two restaurants there are no bars or cafes in Albinen. \nSleep.\nThere are numerous holiday apartments available for rental in Albinen. The commune of Albinen has also set up its own innovative scheme to offer holiday rentals with a hotel-like service that can cater to any needs. For more information visit the Albijou website. "}
{"id": "182381", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182381", "title": "Silvassa", "text": "Silvassa (Gujarati: \u0ab8\u0ac7\u0ab2\u0acd\u0ab5\u0abe\u0ab8 \"Selv\u0101s\") is an industrial town in Western India, wedged between Gujarat and Maharashtra. It is a part of the former Portuguese colony of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu.\nUnderstand.\nUntil the end of the 19th century, Silvassa was only a small village. It started to become more important in 1885, when the Portuguese administration decided to transfer the seat of its Nagar Haveli colony to the city.\nSilvassa hosts many factories and industries providing significant government revenue, allowing the city to maintain a low level of taxation. The town is also popular among Gujaratis for drinking as alcohol is banned in Gujarat.\nThe hospitable, kind, unselfish native local tribal people have their own customs, dances and rituals which are quite fascinating. The city enjoys a very pleasant climate from November to March, which is the best time for a visit. Temperatures during the summer are comfortable too, even during the day, due to the city's proximity to the sea.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nSurat International Airport is 138 km away. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj International Airport is 159 km away.\nBy train.\nVapi is the nearest railhead for getting to Silvassa. People traveling to Silvassa by train can get down at the Vapi and then get there by auto-rickshaws, which ply between the two towns at regular interval.\nBy bus.\nGSRTC buses ply between Silvassa and Vapi at a regular interval.\nBy car.\nSilvassa is connected to Maharashtra and Gujarat via National Highway 848A. Silvassa has a well-maintained road network. Daman is 30 km away via Bhilad on National Highway 48. Mumbai is 160 km away from Silvassa, via Bhilad, on NH 48 Surat is 130 km away from Silvassa, via Bhilad, on NH 48. \nGet around.\nThere are auto-rickshaws and buses available to roam around in the city."}
{"id": "182398", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182398", "title": "Budapest/V\u00e1rosliget", "text": "V\u00e1rosliget (lit. \"City Park\", German: \"Stadtw\u00e4ldchen\") is a large park in Budapest. This article describes the western part of the 14th district: V\u00e1rosliget and Istv\u00e1nmez\u0151.\nUnderstand.\nThe City Park was the main venue of the 1896 millennium celebrations of Hungary, by which time Andr\u00e1ssy Avenue, Millennium Underground and the Grand Boulevard had been built.\nGet in.\n\u00a0M1\u00a0 has three metro stations here: is at the center of City Park, and are located on the edge of the area. \u00a0M2\u00a0 has one metro station located at the southern edge of Istv\u00e1nmez\u0151:.\nTram line \u00a01\u00a0 crosses the area by connecting Kacs\u00f3h Pongr\u00e1c \u00fat, Zugl\u00f3 vas\u00fat\u00e1llom\u00e1s and Pusk\u00e1s Ferenc Stadion. The City Park is well covered by trolleybus lines: \u00a070\u00a0, \u00a072\u00a0, \u00a074\u00a0, \u00a075\u00a0 and \u00a079\u00a0."}
{"id": "182402", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182402", "title": "Maesteg", "text": "Maesteg is a town and community in Bridgend County Borough, Wales. Maesteg lies at the northernmost end of the Llynfi Valley, close to the border with Neath Port Talbot. In 2011, Maesteg had a population of 20,612. The English translation of Maesteg is 'fair field'.\nUnderstand.\nHistorically a part of Glamorgan, the growth of the town started with the opening of ironworks in the 1820s, and 1830s.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nCardiff Airport () is some 20 miles (31 km) to the south east of the town.\nBy train.\nThere is one train station in the centre of town, Maesteg train station is within a 2-minute walk of the town centre. The station is unstaffed.\nIt is a scheduled stop on the hourly service between Maesteg, Bridgend and Cardiff.\nThere are two other stations in Maesteg, one at Ewenny Road and one at Garth, both are unstaffed.\nBy car.\nMaesteg is best accessed by car from Junctions 35 and 36 of the M4 motorway and then going north using the A4063. If arriving from the direction of Swansea the best route is to leave the M4 at junction 41, then take the B4286 to Cwmafan and finally the B4282 to Maesteg.\nThere is a large car park in the centre of town where parking is free of charge.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nMaesteg Bus Station is behind the town hall. First Cymru operate the majority of the services from this station. Services run to Bridgend, Swansea via Port Talbot, Caerau Park, Llangynwyd and Cymmer. "}
{"id": "182403", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=182403", "title": "Llangynwyd", "text": "Llangynwyd is a village 2 miles to the south of Maesteg, in the county borough of Bridgend, Wales. There are two parts to Llangynwyd, the larger more recent village is simply known as Llangynwyd while the smaller much older part which contains St Cynwyds church is locally known as \"Top Llan\". \nUnderstand.\nLlangynwyd was part of the medieval commote (Welsh: \"cwmwd\") of Tir Iarll. Its population was about 3,000 in 2011.\nThe village is the site of Llangynwyd parish church, the ruins of Llangynwyd Castle and one of the oldest pubs in Wales (the Old House, dating from 1147.) \nGet in.\nBy train.\nThere is no train station actually in Llangynwyd; the nearest station is 1.25 km away at.\nBy car.\nLlangynwyd is best accessed by car from Junction 36 of the M4 motorway.\nBy bus.\nFirst Cymru operate the bus services that run through Llangynwyd. \n70 Bridgend-Cymmer\n71 Bridgend-Cymmer\nSleep.\nThere may be other self-catering accommodation in the countryside around the village. You'll find a range of hotels and B&amp;Bs in Bridgend, some 8 miles (13 km) south of Llangynwyd."}
