{"id": "183592", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183592", "title": "Collarenebri", "text": "Collarenebri is a village (population 650 in 2016) in Northwestern New South Wales.\nCollarenebri is in the Walgett Shire Local Government Area.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nCollarenebri is about an hour's drive north east of Walgett via the Gwydir Highway and an hour's drive west of Moree. From Mungindi, take the Gundabloui Road down south west for about an hour."}
{"id": "183593", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183593", "title": "Walgett", "text": "Walgett is a town of 2,145 in northwestern New South Wales.\nWalgett is the main centre of the Walgett Shire Local Government Area.\nGet in.\nWalgett is on the intersection between the Castlereagh Highway, and the Kamilaroi Highways. The town is about 230 km east of Bourke, and 140 km east of Brewarrina, and 215 km WSW of Moree. \nBy bus.\nThe Western NSW coach line leaves from Dubbo every day."}
{"id": "183594", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183594", "title": "Brewarrina", "text": "Brewarrina is a small town with a population 1,651 in 2016 in Northwestern New South Wales. It is known for possibly having the oldest human construction anywhere in the world, with the fish traps being ten times older than Stonehenge, and suits travellers interested in a quieter, lesser known destination, rich in the 65,000 years of Aboriginal history of a sunburnt country.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe town is located amid the traditional lands of the Muruwari, Ngemba, Weilwan and Yualwarri peoples. Brewarrina has a long Indigenous Australian history and was once the meeting ground for over 5,000 people.\nThe first European settlers arrived in the district around 1839\u201340. The first people to own land where the town now stands were the Lawson brothers, who had two holdings - one called \"Walcha\" and another called \"Moona\". The town was first known as \"Walcha Hut\" but this later changed to \"Brewarrina\".\nIn 1859, somewhere between 300 and 400 Aboriginal people were massacred by white settlers in an event known as the Hospital Creek Massacre, recollections of which vary. A memorial was erected by the local Aboriginal Land Council near the site of the massacre.\nIn 1859 a riverboat called Gemini, skippered by William Randell, reached the town. This opened the possibility of developing the town as a port, and by the early 1860s Brewarrina was recognised as the furthest navigable point on the Darling River. Brewarrina became a port for shipping wool to Adelaide via the Darling and Murray rivers. The town was formally surveyed and laid out in 1861 and proclaimed on 28 April 1863.\nThe paddle steamer Wandering Jew of 66 tonnes, 22 \u00d7 4.4 \u00d7 1.5 m, was built in 1866 and registered at Sydney. On 15 December 1914, Wandering Jew was lost due to a fire on Barwon River. \"The \"Wandering Jew\" represents an earlier maritime era and provides a direct link to the riverine heritage of Brewarrina. Its colourful history and repeated damage by fire is evocative of the dramas associated with riverboat travel\".\nThe 1870s were something of a boom time for Brewarrina. The courthouse was built in 1871. The Telegraph reached town in 1873. The Mechanics Institute formed in 1873. The following year two hotels, two stores and the Commercial Bank all opened, and in 1875 the Parish of Brewarrina was formed and public school was opened. All this development was largely due to Cobb and Co, which had a number of coach services passing through the town. There was a service from Byrock, one from Dubbo via Warren and, in 1874, a direct service from Brewarrina to Enngonia, north of Bourke. The number of people moving through the town at this time would have been considerable and would have given rise to the increase in stores and hotels.\nThe Barwon Bridge opened in 1888, the previous method of crossing the Barwon River was by punt and pontoon. The impetus for Brewarrina bridge, was to capture the New South Wales wool trade from the river paddle steamers and direct it away from Melbourne and Adelaide to Sydney. It is a rare bridge because it, and the lift bridge at North Bourke, are the only surviving examples of the first series of lift bridges in New South Wales. The bridge has been assessed as being of state significance and is listed on the New South Wales State Heritage Register.\nIn 1901 the Brewarrina railway line opened to Brewarrina from Byrock, on the Nyngan to Bourke line. The Brewarrina Line closed in 1974, and the wood-framed Brewarrina Station burned to the ground in 1980. The local telephone exchange was established in 1913. The town was surveyed in 1920. Brewarrina was used as a location for the Australian silent film Moora Neya, or The Message of the Spear (1911). \nThe Brewarrina Ngemba Billabong has a strong cultural history. From 1876 to 1967 the Ngemba Billabong was the Brewarrina Aboriginal Mission for local Aboriginal people whose land was taken for grazing. The entire 261 hectare property is listed on the NSW State Heritage Register. The Brewarrina Aboriginal Mission was the oldest institutional-type community in the state, it ran until 1965. Brewarrina Mission was the first institution formally established by the Aborigines Protection Board as part of its policy to segregate Aboriginal people.\nIn August 1987 Brewarrina erupted into a riot that was triggered by the death in police custody of Lloyd James Boney. This coincided with 10 August 1987 when the Prime Minister Bob Hawke announced a Royal Commission into Aboriginal Deaths in Custody.\nClimate.\nBrewarrina has the typical hot semi-arid climate of north-western New South Wales, with hot summers frequently over 40 \u00b0C, cool winters and generally dry all year round. Brewarrina's highest recorded temperature was 48.9 \u00b0C, whilst its coldest was \u22124.1 \u00b0C and the average annual rainfall is 412.2 mm.\nGet in.\nThe only way you can get in to Brewarrina is by car. From Brisbane, take the M1 down, and exit at B76, and continue following it for about 900km. You could also use the M2, A2, A39 and B76 from Brisbane which is a lot shorter in distance; but more winding.\nFrom Sydney, you'd go the way you'd normally go to Dubbo, but then keep following A32 until Nyngan. Instead of continuing on A32, instead go straight onto B71 until Coolabah. From Coolabah, follow Arthur Hall VC Way, until Brewarrina.\nHowever, it isn't too far from nearby Bourke:\nAirlink fly to Bourke from Dubbo. Flights to Dubbo are available from Sydney and some other regional locations.\nNSW Trainlink have a daily train service to Dubbo from Sydney connecting with a bus service to Bourke. The trip will take a full day's travel.\nBut from here, you'll need to drive.\nGet around.\nThe town is pretty small; enough that you can basically walk throughout the town. It's mostly flat, but it can get quite hot in the summer. Taking the car out might not be ideal, since Brewarrina is quite an old town, and that means the streets are quite narrow. It's best to come to Brewarrina, and park your car, and only take it when you're going out of Brewarrina again.\nSee.\nBrewarrina is particularly known for its rich Indigenous Aboriginal heritage. It's also famous for having the world's oldest human construction, anywhere in the world (most think this is ten times older than Stonehenge but the actual date is still unknown). While Brewarrina is often quite underrated for its rich Aboriginal heritage, that doesn't mean that it's not a tourist spot at all, but in fact, Brewarrina is quite popular with frequent outbackers, and it's a beauty in its own right.\nBuy.\nMany will wonder, what's actually unique to buy in a small tiny rural town with only a mere couple of thousand people. And the answer is quite different than you think. Since this town is rich in Aboriginal culture, there's a lot of authentic Aboriginal items you can probably not easily buy in big and major cities, or even major region cities like Dubbo or Wagga. \nEat.\nIsolation and the general smallness of most rural towns hinder them from having abundant eating options. Brewarrina can get you lost in time though.\nDrink.\nThere are only two pubs in Brewarrina, and the RSL also has some alcoholic drinks as well.\nConnect.\nMobile coverage.\nTelstra has full strong 4G and 3G coverage in Brewarrina, with an approximate 20km radius out of Brewarrina. Optus and Vodafone have no coverage at all and thus, if you're using Optus or Vodafone, bring a satellite phone with you. \nInternet.\nThere's free Wi-Fi in the visitor centre."}
{"id": "183595", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183595", "title": "Coonabarabran", "text": "Coonabarabran is a small town (population 3,477 in 2021) in the Central West region of New South Wales, Australia.\nCoonabarabran is the main centre of the Warrumbungle Shire Local Government Area.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nCoonabarabran is at the very end of B56, and is located on the Newell Highway. The town is about a two hour drive from Dubbo north via the A39 Newell Highway and is a 1.5 hour drive south from Narrabri via the A39 Newell Highway.\nBy plane.\nThough () exists, there are no regularly scheduled flights to the airport. The nearest airports with regular services are in Dubbo to the southwest or Tamworth to the east."}
{"id": "183600", "revid": "692", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183600", "title": "Strzelecki Track", "text": "The Strzelecki Track (D96) is an outback track from Lyndhurst to Innamincka in South Australia.\nUnderstand.\nThe Strzelecki Track is one of SA's four major tracks along with the Birdsville, Oodnadatta, and Borefield Tracks. Although there are nine major tracks, these four have been proclaimed as more significant. Whilst all of these are unpaved, they still are tracks which \"must\" be used to access various different towns across South Australia.\nWith a total length of 475 kilometres, the Strzelecki Track is not very long as Australian standards go -- however, you will need a 4WD to get from Lyndhurst to Innamincka. But what it lacks is the number of servos along the way: zero.\nAdditionally, while some maps may have the marker \"D96\" on it, the route is unsigned, and thus, don't go looking for D96 signs due to the DPTI's policy of unsigning unsealed roads.\nPrepare.\nThis is remote country. People have died from exposure not far from the main track. Take extra food and water. Hire a satellite phone and take an additional spare tyre.\nCheck the road conditions with the Government of South Australia, and check the local noticeboards at either end or call 1300 361 033. Check the conditions before you start on the track, and again before you set out each day. The road conditions can change rapidly and the track can be closed to 2WD or 4WD traffic at any time of year. If it rains, and you are in a 2WD car, you should wait for the track to dry rather than have the unpleasant experience of battling the track in the wet.\nGet in.\nThe Strzelecki Track can be only accessed at either end and at Moomba.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nThe only way you will manage to get on this track is by a 4WD. Do not attempt to bring a sedan, caravan or anything that is not a 4WD as it will almost certainly struggle to climb up the high mountains of the Flinders Ranges.\nBy bus.\nBuses do not operate on this track and will not operate on this route in the near future due to safety reasons.\nGo.\nThe drive will take at least two days with 475kms long distances without fuel. \nLyndhurst to Mt. Hopeless.\nAlong the way\nThe track starts or finishes here, depending on which side you're coming from. Regardless, if you're starting here Lyndhurst will be your only source of fuel here, as if you forget to fuel up here, you're pretty much screwed, unless you realise it before hitting 50km before where you started.\nAs the name indicates, most of this route is unpaved, but unlike some other outback tracks, even experienced 4WD drivers will find this tough. It is never a good idea to go without a satellite phone, and if you see anyone stuck... stop and help them. Share your supplies.\nThis is also where the Flinders Ranges crossing is, and thus, the climb is only for the experienced. It isn't advisable to do this section until you've gone 1000km of unsealed driving.\nHere, you'll also find a noticeboard on which sections are open, and which aren't. Carefully take a look, since if one section is closed, then you might be stuck for weeks on end.\nMt. Hopeless to Merty Merty.\nAlong the way\nMerty Merty to Moomba.\nAlong the way\nMoomba to Innamincka.\nAlong the way\nA shorter route is available via a public access road between Moomba and Innamincka, making the distance 458 km (285 mi) and not 475km.\nStay safe.\nAlways have at least four 1L water bottles and an esky (to keep the bottles cool) with you at all times. It \"will\" get really hot and dry all year around. Additionally, bring some jackets as it can get to freezing temperatures at night. Do not use this track at night and do not proceed without a spare tyre with you. Additionally, bring a tyre pump with you as only 0.2% of the track is paved.\nConnect.\nZero percent of the route is covered by mobile signal. A satellite phone is a must and going without one could mean death."}
{"id": "183601", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183601", "title": "Burren Junction", "text": "Burren Junction is a hamlet of 225 (2021) in Northwestern New South Wales. It is in the Walgett Shire Local Government Area.\nUnderstand.\nBurren Junction started as a railway encampment in 1902.\nGet in.\nAlthough there is a railway line, you will only be travelling on it if you're a shipment of wheat. There is an airstrip at Burren Junction Airport, but no scheduled flights. \nBy coach.\nThere are two \"coaches\" from Narrabri via Wee Waa, one on Friday morning and one during the evening. Despite NSW Trainlink calling it a coach, it operately functions more as a minibus than a coach. Fares cost per adult each way."}
{"id": "183603", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183603", "title": "Monticello (Mississippi)", "text": "Monticello is a town of 1,500 people (2019) on the Pearl River in Lawrence County, south Mississippi. Monticello is home to several beautiful historic buildings on the National Register of Historic Places. \nUnderstand.\nMonticello was founded in 1798 and named for the home of Thomas Jefferson. The town's name is pronounced with a soft 'c' sound rather than the 'ch' sound used for the home of Thomas Jefferson. \nAround 1810, Joseph Cooper started ferry service across the Pearl River in Monticello. Beginning around 1812, St. Stephens Road ran from Natchez, Mississippi to St Stephens, Alabama crossing the Pearl River at Monticello. As Natchez was a prominent city at the time, Monticello gained perhaps its greatest claim to fame in 1821. The Mississippi legislature selected Monticello to be the state capital. However this honor was to be short lived as the legislature reconsidered and picked Jackson the very next day. \nPrior to 1860, Monticello was important economically, as the Pearl River was one of the chief north-south transportation routes through Mississippi. However, the main railroad was built through Brookhaven diverting much traffic about 20 miles to the east. Despite the advent of the railroad and its construction east of the river, the Pearl River continued to be an important waterway. Between 1880 and 1922, a shipping channel was maintained in the Pearl River as far north as Jackson, allowing Monticello to remain on a major thoroughfare. \nA devastating tornado tore through Monticello in April 1882 nearly obliterating every building. After the tornado, Monticello was rebuilt over the next several years. \nThe main industries in Monticello and Lawrence County are based on agriculture and pine trees, although there is manufacturer of large excavator buckets in Monticello as well.\nGet in.\nThere is no public transportation to Monticello since the passenger train depot closed more than 50 years ago; Monticello has never been serviced by intercity buses.\nBy car.\nMonticello can be accessed by car via US 84 and several state highways. US 84 runs through Mississippi from Natchez to Laurel via Brookhaven and Collins. Monticello is between Brookhaven and Collins. A bypass around Monticello opened in 2006 and Monticello can now be accessed by two interchanges, one at Mississippi Highway 27 and one at Old Highway 27. The old highway 84 runs straight through the town and is signed as 184. Mississippi Highway 27 runs north/south through Monticello. If you are traveling south from Jackson, Highway 27 crosses Interstate 55 at Crystal Springs and provides convenient access to Monticello via Mississippi highway 184. Smaller state highways also service Monticello; 587 serves Monticello from the south toward the small communities of White Bluff and Morgantown and originates farther south in Foxworth near Columbia. Highway 43 runs from Columbia north to Monticello on the east side of the Pearl River via Oakvale and Arm. Access to Monticello from the community of New Hebron in northeast part of Lawrence County is also provided by Highway 43.\nGet around.\nAs there is no public transportation in Monticello, the best ways to get around Monticello are by car and by foot. The core of Monticello is quite small and can be easily walked, Atwood Water Park and the two motels are a little farther away (each about 1 mile in different directions). This doesn't present too big a barrier, except that Atwood Water Park is across the Pearl River and the bridge over the river is particularly unfriendly for pedestrians. \nDo.\nThe Town of Monticello operates several parks and two ball parks. Cooper's Ferry Park is just north of the Civic Center, while Atwood Water Park is a half mile east of the town center across the Pearl River. Atwood Water Park features camping opportunities as well as a boat ramp in the Pearl.\nBuy.\nWhile Monticello is small, it still has several nice places to shop. While many of these places specialize in used items, there is still a wide variety of stuff available. \nEat.\nMonticello is home to a variety of local dining options as well as a number of well-known chains. \nDrink.\nWhile alcoholic beverages are legal within the city limits of Monticello, only beer and light wine are legal throughout the rest of Lawrence County. Any wine or liquor purchased in Monticello cannot be legally taken into the surrounding county. There are no bars in Monticello, although there is a local Coffee shop and two liquor stores. \nSleep.\nThe Town of Monticello is served by two motels near the intersection of Mississippi Highway 27 and Highway 184 in the western part of Monticello. There are also camping opportunities at Atwood Water Park and Lake Mary Crawford. Atwood Water Park has tent camping, cabins, and full RV hookups."}
{"id": "183608", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183608", "title": "Zhoukou", "text": "Zhoukou (\u5468\u53e3 Zh\u014duk\u01d2u) is in East Henan\uff0con the Shaying River. It is known for its long-standing role as a regional transport and trading hub. The city's origins trace back to ancient settlements around the states of Chen and Cai, two historical polities active during the Zhou Dynasty. During the Han and Tang periods (roughly 200 BCE\u2013900 CE), the region developed further as agriculture and river trade expanded. Zhoukou remained a collection of counties for most of its history and did not form a unified prefecture-level city until 2000. Today, Zhoukou is a steadily growing regional center with strong agricultural, commercial, and cultural significance.\nUnderstand.\nZhoukou (\u5468\u53e3) is a city located in eastern Henan Province, surrounded by Zhumadian, Xuchang, Luohe, Kaifeng, Shangqiu, and Anhui Province. With over 9 million residents, Zhoukou serves as an important city in the Central Plains region.\nZhoukou is known as the birthplace of Laozi, the founder of Taoism, and has deep cultural roots connected to the ancient states of Chen and Chu. Visitors interested in Chinese history and traditional philosophy will find many sites related to these legacies.\nThe city lies where the Sha River and Ying River meet, giving it the nickname \u201cCentral Plains Port City.\u201d The rivers have played a major role in Zhoukou\u2019s development as a transportation and trade center.\nModern Zhoukou continues to grow, with the Lin\u2019gang New City area showing the city\u2019s rapid urbanization. The convenient network of highways, railways, and waterways makes travel to and from Zhoukou easy, and also provides a good base for exploring eastern Henan and neighboring Anhui.\nOrientation.\nZhoukou administers:\nHistory.\nAccording to ancient geographical records such as the Shang,\nZhoukou developed along the Huai River basin and became an important cultural and agricultural center in early Chinese history. The area played a significant role during the Shang and Zhou periods, with Huaiyang identified as one of the core ritual and settlement areas in eastern Henan.\nDuring the Qin and Han dynasties, administrative units such as Chen County (Chenxian) and Huaiyang County were formally established, forming the early governmental structure of the region. These counties served as regional hubs and contributed to the development of local culture and economy.\nIn the Ming Dynasty, the national administrative reform established the three-tier system of provinces, prefectures, and counties. At that time, the region of present-day Zhoukou was governed under Chenzhou Prefecture (Chenzhou Fu), with counties including Huaiyang, Chen, Taikang, and Shenqiu forming the core administrative units.\nIn 2000, Zhoukou was officially designated as a prefecture-level city.\nCulture.\nZhoukou is one of the important cradles of Chinese civilization and the cultural center of the ancient Chen and Cai states. It is closely associated with the legendary figure Fuxi, regarded as one of the earliest cultural ancestors of China. The region is also considered by many scholars to be one of the birthplaces of early clan systems and ritual traditions.\nTo the northeast of the city lies the Taihao Mausoleum, dedicated to Fuxi and historically revered as a major site of ancestral worship. Along with the N\u00fcwa Mausoleum and the Huaguantai site\u2014associated with some of the earliest forms of oracle bone inscriptions\u2014this area reflects Zhoukou\u2019s deep cultural roots in mythology, early writing, and state formation.\nZhoukou also preserves the heritage of the ancient Chen and Cai cultures, including the ruins of the Chen Kingdom capital and the Cai Kingdom capital, both influential centers during the Zhou Dynasty. The region has produced notable historical figures such as Yin Xi, the mentor of Laozi; Chen Ping, the strategist of the Han Dynasty; and Fang Zhongduo, a Republican-era general known for his bravery.\nAs home to multiple national-level protected heritage sites, including the Taihao Mausoleum, the Huaguantai remains, and the ruins of the Chen and Cai ancient cities, Zhoukou plays a significant role in the study of early Chinese civilization, ritual culture, and political development.\nClimate.\nZhoukou is located in the transition zone between the warm temperate region and the northern subtropical region, and it experiences a typical continental monsoon climate. Influenced by solar radiation, terrain, hydrology, and large-scale atmospheric circulation, the city features a moderate overall climate with four distinct seasons, hot and humid summers, and cold, dry winters. The main characteristics of Zhoukou\u2019s climate include: dry and windy springs, hot and rainy summers, mild and clear autumns, and cold winters with relatively little precipitation.\nWinter is the longest season in Zhoukou, followed by summer, while spring and autumn are comparatively shorter. Based on long-term climatic observations, spring in most parts of Zhoukou typically begins in late March and ends in mid-May. Summer lasts from late May to early September. Autumn spans from early September to early November, and winter extends from mid-November to the end of March of the following year.\nAcross Zhoukou\u2019s counties and districts, the annual average temperature generally rises gradually from north to south as the terrain becomes flatter and more open. The city\u2019s annual mean temperature is approximately 14.5\u00b0C to 15.1\u00b0C. The highest recorded temperature reached about 41\u00b0C, while the extreme lowest temperature recorded was around \u221217\u00b0C, with most urban monitoring sites showing similar minimum values during severe winter cold waves.\nZhoukou receives an annual average precipitation of about 700 millimeters, with most rainfall concentrated from June to September. The frost-free period averages around 215\u2013220 days each year, and the city typically records approximately 2,200 to 2,300 hours of sunshine annually.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nZhoukou East Station \u2013 Xinzheng Airport\nBy bus.\nThere are four long-distance bus stations in Zhoukou City: Zhoukou East Bus Station, Huaiyang Bus Station, Zhoukou Central Bus Station, and Zhoukou Bus Terminal. Among these, Zhoukou East Bus Station, Huaiyang Bus Station, and Zhoukou Central Bus Station have the most frequent departures. Buses serve nearly every destination within Henan Province. While buses may not be as comfortable as trains, they are more affordable and avoid the crowded conditions often found on trains. Seats are guaranteed, though buses may depart only after reaching full capacity rather than strictly following a timetable. For long-distance journeys, some buses may feature sleeper compartments instead of standard seating.\nGet around.\nThe city's focal point is Wuyi Square (\u4e94\u4e00\u5e7f\u573a). This large public square becomes lively in the evening, when vendors set up stalls and locals gather to socialize. From the square, several main streets extend to different parts of the city.\nAs Zhoukou has developed gradually as a regional center rather than being a historically large city, its urban layout is functional and practical, with open streets, green spaces, and a relatively logical street arrangement.\nZhoukou is moderately spread out, but most attractions and neighborhoods are accessible by public bus. Bus fares are typically \u00a51 or \u00a52 per ride, depending on whether the bus has air-conditioning, with A/C buses being slightly more expensive but usually less crowded.\nBy Taix.\nTaxi fares in Zhoukou begin at \u00a56 for the first 2 km during the day, and \u00a57 late at night (23:00\u201305:00). Each kilometer after the first 2 km costs \u00a51.6. For trips longer than 9 km, the fare for the excess distance increases by 50% to \u00a52.4 per km. When the taxi speed drops below 12 km/h, a time-based charge applies: the first 5 minutes are free, then \u00a51 for every subsequent 5 minutes. All taxis are metered, but passengers should be aware that some drivers may overcharge.\nDrivers of local taxis and private cars sometimes use the phrase \"Going or not?\" \uff08 '\u8d70\u4e0d\u8d70'\uff0cPinyin: z\u014du b\u01d4 z\u014du\uff09to pick up passengers.\nDo.\nWuyi Square (\u4e94\u4e00\u5e7f\u573a) is the central hub of Zhoukou, where locals gather in the evenings to socialize, enjoy street food, and explore small vendor markets. The square is surrounded by several main streets that fan out to different parts of the city, making it a convenient starting point to explore Zhoukou\u2019s neighborhoods. While Zhoukou is not a major tourist city, Wuyi Square offers a lively and authentic glimpse into local daily life.\nFestivals.\n2024: The first edition opened on 11 March 2024, featuring participation from more than 30 publishing brands.\n2025: The second edition opened on 8 March 2025 and extended until 30 March 2025, with continued involvement from cultural and publishing organizations.\nDates: Held from 18 July to 18 September each year.\nVisitors can observe seasonal lotus blooms and attend various local cultural activities.\nDrink.\nBeverage shops in Zhoukou are mainly concentrated in the commercial streets and shopping malls of Chuanhui District. Options include milk tea shops, fruit drink outlets, and caf\u00e9s, most of which offer takeout service. Opening hours typically focus on daytime and early evening. Chain brands are common across the urban area and serve as convenient stops for a short break or quick refreshment during travel.\nConnect.\nInternet.\nMost hotels, guesthouses, caf\u00e9s, and shopping malls in Zhoukou offer free Wi-Fi, though the connection speed may vary. Mobile data coverage is generally stable within the urban districts, including Chuanhui, Huaiyang, and Shangshui.\nMobile.\nChina\u2019s major mobile carriers \u2014 China Mobile, China Unicom, and China Telecom \u2014 all provide good coverage in Zhoukou. SIM cards can be purchased at official carrier stores or kiosks with a passport.\nChina Mobile: Wide coverage, strong 4G signal in both downtown and county areas.\nChina Unicom: Good 4G speeds, often cheaper data packages.\nChina Telecom: Strong in urban Zhoukou; some rural areas may vary.\n5G service is available in most central districts.\nInternet censorship.\nAccess to foreign websites such as Google, Instagram, YouTube, WhatsApp, and many news services is restricted. A VPN is required to access these platforms, though VPN legality and functionality may vary.\nPost.\nChina Post offices are found throughout Zhoukou, including major branches in Chuanhui District. Delivery services are generally reliable for domestic mail; international mail may take longer. Private couriers such as SF Express, YTO, and ZTO are widely used for parcel shipments.\nGo next.\nHenan offers many nearby destinations accessible by long-distance buses from Zhoukou:"}
{"id": "183613", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183613", "title": "Texas (Queensland)", "text": "Texas is a village (population 843 in 2016) in the Darling Downs region of Queensland in Australia\nTexas is in the Goondiwindi Region Local Government Area.\nGet in.\nTexas is on State Road 89 (Texas Rd). From Stanthorpe, the nearest minor regional centre, travel about 100 km west (should take about 65\u201370 minutes). The road generally lacks a shoulder, but the road conditions are still mostly suitable to drive 100 km/h.\nFrom Inglewood, Texas is a 55 km drive south (should take roughly 35\u201340 min)."}
{"id": "183621", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183621", "title": "Remagen", "text": "Remagen is a small town in Rhineland Palatinate which was propelled to world fame during World War II when a railway bridge the Wehrmacht had only managed to partially destroy fell into American hands and allowed the Western Allies to cross the Rhine into the German heartland. A famous 1969 film based on the battle titled \"The Bridge at Remagen\" ensured the enduring fame of the battle, the bridge and the town.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nRemagen station is served by local, regional and some long-distance train services. Regional service \"RE 5\" starts in Wesel and stops at various places along the Rhine River, including Duisburg, Duesseldorf Airport, Duesseldorf, Cologne and Bonn. After Remagen it carries on south to Koblenz. \"RE26\" serves all local stops between Cologne and Mainz along the left bank of the Rhine. The journey from Cologne takes about 80-90 minutes. \"RB30\" and \"RB39\" branch off into the Ahr Valley (Ahrtal) to the south of here, via the Ahrtalbahn, which is still damaged from the deadly 2021 floods. A few IC and ICE services stop here as well. \nBy car.\nAutobahn A61, Exit 31 Dreieck Sinzig. Or follow the beautiful Rhine Valley on Highway B9 which passes through many of the small towns along the river between Bonn and Koblenz.\nGet around.\nThe town is small enough that all sights are easily accessible on foot. The walk between Remagen Station and the river bank is quite a descent, thus also a steep hill to walk back up again. "}
{"id": "183623", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183623", "title": "Dargo", "text": "Dargo is a small town in Gippsland, Victoria, Australia.\nGet in.\nThe only way to get into Dargo is by car. If you're coming from an eastern direction (Bairnsdale, Lakes Entrance, Orbost) Dargo can be accessed using the C601 route which forks off at Bairnsdale from the A1 route. From the western direction (Melbourne, Traralgon, Sale) leave the A1 route in Stratford, and follow the C494 route until Stockdale Road, and then drive down Stockdale Road to the end, where it forks off into two roads. Follow Beverleys Road, which ends on the C601 route. From there, you can just go up the C601 route until you reach Dargo. The road north of Dargo is closed in winter.\nEat.\nThe only restaurant in Dargo are at the Dargo Hotel (listed in drink).\nConnect.\nThe only mobile coverage in Dargo is from Telstra on the 4G network. A satellite phone may be wise if you're going on the Wonnagatta Drive."}
{"id": "183625", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183625", "title": "Willow Tree", "text": "Willow Tree is a village (population 308 in 2016) in the southernmost part of New England region of New South Wales in Australia.\nUnderstand.\nWillow Tree is located in what was the substantial land allocation named Warrah which was granted to the Australian Agricultural Company in 1833. An inn was established on the site where the road from the Hunter Valley branched north to Quirindi and north-east to Wallabadah and Tamworth. The site of the settlement and the first blocks of land sold in 1908. The railway station was opened as Warrah in 1877, but renamed to Willow Tree two years later. Willow Tree is a popular break for highway travellers.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nWillow Tree is located on the New England Highway (A15), approximately 1 km south of the junction of the New England Highway and the Kamilaroi Highway (B51).\nWillow Tree is 19 km north-west of Murrurundi via the New England Highway, and it is 74 km south-west of Tamworth also via the New England Highway.\nWillow Tree is 18 km south-east of Quirindi via the Kamilaroi Highway/B51.\nBy train.\nTrains run from Sydney daily to Willow Tree, and on to Werris Creek, where lines branch off to Tamworth and Armidale and to Narrabri and Moree. \nTrains depart Sydney at Central Station M-Su 9:30AM and arrive at Willow Tree 2:25PM. Trains depart Willow Tree M-Su 11:41AM and arrive at Central Station in Sydney 4:39PM.\nA timetable and map can be downloaded here. \nGet around.\nPrivate transport is essential, as there is no taxi service or a local bus service in Willow Tree.\nThe nearest car rental agencies are in Quirindi, Werris Creek, and Tamworth.\nIt will take less than fifteen minutes to cycle from one end of the town to the other.\nMake sure you slow down to 70 km/h through the town centre \u2013 there is a speed camera in the centre of town.\nBuy.\nLocals do their essential shopping in Quirindi, and what they can't get there they go to Tamworth to shop.\nCope.\nBanks and ATMs.\nThere are no banks or ATMs in Willow Tree. It is advisable to do any necessary banking and to obtain cash before departing for Willow Tree, and to use EFTPOS whenever possible while there."}
{"id": "183653", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183653", "title": "Cheyyar", "text": "Cheyyar is in Northern Tamil Nadu.\nUnderstand.\nCheyyar also often referred to as Tiruvetipuram in legal documents. It is a well-known town about 160 km south of Chennai, the capital city of Tamil Nadu.\nGet in.\nIt is well connected to other cities in the state by state and private transportation buses."}
{"id": "183654", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183654", "title": "Werris Creek", "text": "Werris Creek is a village in the southernmost part of the New England region of New South Wales in Australia.\nUnderstand.\nThe indigenous Kamileroi people were the original inhabitants of the area. The name 'Werris' is thought to be derived from the Kamilaroi word 'werai' meaning 'a lookout point' in reference to the many hills in the district. European settlers arrived here in the 1830s. The heritage-listed railway station was built in the late 1880s, and Werris Creek is considered to be the first railway town in Australia.\nWerris Creek is in the Liverpool Plains Local Government Area, with its main centre in Quirindi. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nWerris Creek is located on the Werris Creek Road, 20 km north of Quirindi and 46 km south-west of Tamworth.\nWerris Creek is 35 km north-west (via Quirindi on the Kamilaroi Highway) of the junction of the New England Highway/A15 and the Kamilaroi Highway/B51, which is just north of Willow Tree.\nBy bus.\nTamworth Buslines.\nTamworth Buslines run Bus Route 428 M-Sa to Werris Creek. This is a regional bus service, not a coach service - pay the driver with cash as you would a city bus service, exact fare preferred. One-way fares between Werris Creek and Tamworth are $5.90 for adults and $2.90 for children (4-15) and concession holders.\nTo Werris Creek.\nThe morning service to Werris Creek departs from The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue M-F 9:05AM and arrives at the Werris Creek Post Office 9:44AM. The afternoon services depart from Tamworth Railway Station at M-F 2PM, then departs from The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue 2:02PM and arrives at the Werris Creek Railway Station 2:42PM; and departs The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue M-F 5:17PM and arrives at the Werris Creek Post Office 5:54PM.\nOn Saturdays the service to Werris Creek departs from The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue Sa 10AM and arrives at the Werris Creek Post Office 10:40AM; departs from The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue 1:30PM and arrives at the Werris Creek Post Office 2:10PM; and departs from The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue Sa 4PM and arrives at the Werris Creek Post Office 4:40PM\nTo Tamworth.\nThe morning service to Tamworth departs from the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance M-F 7:15 AM and arrives at the corner of Brisbane Street and Kabel Avenue 7:50AM; and departs from the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance M-F 11:59AM, arrives at the corner of Brisbane Street and Kabel Avenue 12:38PM, and arrives at the Tamworth Railway Station 12:40PM; and departs from the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance M-F 2:21PM and arrives at Adairs at the corner of Brisbane Street and Kable Avenue 2:54PM.\nOn Saturdays the service to Tamworth departs from the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance Sa 9:14AM and arrives at The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue 9:53AM; departs the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance Sa 11:49AM and arrives at The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue 12:28PM; and departs from the Werris Creek Railway pedestrian entrance Sa 3:14PM and arrives at The Atrium Shopping Centre in Kabel Avenue1 3:53PM.\nBy train.\nTrains run from Sydney daily to Werris Creek, where lines branch off to Tamworth and Armidale and to Narrabri and Moree. \nTrains depart Sydney at Central Station daily 9:30AM and arrive at Werris Creek 2:57PM. Trains depart Werris Creek daily 11:07AM and arrive at Central Station in Sydney 4:39PM.\nA timetable and map can be downloaded here. \nTrain connections.\nOnce the train reaches Werris Creek, it splits into two - one section heads north-west to Quirindi, Gunnedah, Boggabri, and Narrabri, and terminates at Moree. The other section heads north-east to Tamworth, Walcha Road (the nearest railway station for Walcha), Uralla, and terminates at Armidale.\nTravellers who wish to travel on to those destinations or to Sydney, or who wish to travel between stations on the Gunnedah-Narrabri-Moree branch line and the Tamworth-Armidale branch line by transferring at Werris Creek will need to make their bookings before departure, as there is no longer a ticket office at the Werris Creek Railway Station, there is no ticketing agent in Werris Creek, and tickets are not available on the trains themselves.\nAlso note that the Walcha Road Railway Station is not in Walcha, but 24 km north-west of Walcha. A taxi-link service run by Trainlink is available between Walcha Road Railway Station and Walcha itself, but that too must be booked as part of your journey before departure.\nFrom Tamworth and Armidale.\nTrains depart Werris Creek daily 2:57PM and arrive at Tamworth 3:37 PM, at Walcha Road 4:46PM, at Uralla 5:12PM, and terminate at Armidale at 5:35PM.\nTrains depart Armidale daily 8:40AM, and arrive at Uralla 8:56AM, at Walcha Road at 9:22AM, at Tamworth 10:27AM, and at Werris Creek 11:07AM to meet the other train section.\nTo/from Gunnedah-Narrabri-Moree.\nTrains depart Werris Creek M-Su 2:57PM, arrive at Gunnedah at 3:45PM, at Boggabri 4:10PM, at Narrabri 4:52PM, and terminate at Moree 6PM.\nTrains depart Moree M-Su 8:05AM, arrive at Narrabri 8:45AM, at Boggabri 9:47AM, at Gunnedah 10:14AM, and at Werris Creek 11:05AM to meet with the other train section.\nBy plane.\nWerris Creek is not served by any domestic flights. The nearest airport with regular scheduled services in is Tamworth.\nGet around.\nPrivate transport is the most convenient way to get around Werris Creek, and essential for exploring the surrounding district. \nThe nearest car rental agencies are in Tamworth and Quirindi.\nBuy.\nWhen locals can't obtain anything locally, they shop at Quirindi and often at Tamworth.\nCope.\nBanks and ATMs.\nThere are no major banks in Werris Creek, and there are no ATMs. It is advisable to do any necessary banking and to obtain cash before departing for Werris Creek, and to use EFTPOS there whenever possible."}
{"id": "183656", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183656", "title": "Menindee", "text": "Menindee is a minor town (population 537 in 2021) in the Far West region of New South Wales.\nMenindee is in the Central Darling Shire Local Government Area, whose main centre is Wilcannia.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMenindee is around 1.25 hrs away from Broken Hill via Menindee Road. Note that Broken Hill observes ACST and ACDT while Menindee like the rest of New South Wales follows AEST and AEDT. You may have an extra half an hour to spare or you might lose half an hour, depending on which direction you're going.\nBy train.\n is served by the Western NSW 446 Central train service from Broken Hill and takes about 1 hour and 9 minutes. It is also served by the Western NSW 445 Broken Hill train service from Central and takes about 12 hours and 4 minutes.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nCar is a quick way to go around the town but the time spent isn't worth the time to turn on, and turn off the car quickly. You'd be better off walking unless you are going out of town.\nBy walking.\nWalking is also a great option and the quickest point from end to end is 3 km long. If you choose to walk in the summer months, it can get up to 60\u02daC. Drink sufficient water and always carry water with you.\nConnect.\nTelstra fully covers the area with either 3G or 4G. Other providers don't provide coverage in Menindee. Note that this area uses the 08 phone numbering code and not the 02 used in the eastern parts of the state."}
{"id": "183657", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183657", "title": "Beaudesert", "text": "Beaudesert is a town in the South East Queensland region of Queensland in Australia.\nBeaudesert is the main centre of the Scenic Rim Local Government Area. \nUnderstand.\nBeaudesert takes its name from a pastoral station, established in 1842 by an early resident.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Brisbane, head south west onto National Route 13 for about 45 mins, and you'll arrive at Beaudesert."}
{"id": "183664", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183664", "title": "Ste. Rose du Lac", "text": "Ste. Rose du Lac is a small town of around 1,000 people (2021) in the Prairie Mountain region of Manitoba.\nUnderstand.\nSte. Rose du Lac is an unincorporated community that had been an incorporated town before it amalgamated with the surrounding rural municipality to form the Municipality of Ste. Rose. Nestled between Lake Dauphin and Lake Manitoba, Ste. Rose offers various summer and winter recreation facilities. The area is ideally suited for cattle ranching and Ste. Rose du Lac was declared \"Cattle Capital of Manitoba\" in 1979. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nSte. Rose du Lac is located at the intersection of PTH 5 &amp; 68, 50 km east of the city of Dauphin and 300 km northwest of Winnipeg."}
{"id": "183665", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183665", "title": "St. Laurent (Manitoba)", "text": "The Rural Municipality of St. Laurent is located in the Interlake region of Manitoba, Canada. A sparsely-populated area on the southeastern shore of Lake Manitoba, it includes the communities of St. Laurent and Oak Point.\nUnderstand.\nSt. Laurent is a historically-M\u00e9tis settlement and is one of the few remaining places in which the Michif language (a M\u00e9tis language which is a combination of Cree and French) is still spoken. St. Laurent has no central town square and is not exclusively organized around central roads, which is partially the result of the influence of the seigneurial system of New France, which allotted land with respect to the waterfront.\nHistory.\nSt. Laurent was established as Fond du Lac in 1824 by M\u00e9tis leaving Pembina, North Dakota. Pembina, located in Rupert's Land, had been recently ceded by Great Britain to the United States via the Treaty of 1818, prompting the departure of the M\u00e9tis there. More settlers arrived in 1826 as a result of flooding of the Red River of the North, with further growth driven by M\u00e9tis in search of land and traders seeking to take advantage of trade routes to the northwest. The economy centred around fishing and the fur trade, with settlers serving as intermediaries with Cree and Assiniboine people.\nSometime after its establishment as a parish in 1858, the community was renamed St. Laurent, either after a Catholic priest who established the permanent mission there or after the martyr St. Lawrence. Additional M\u00e9tis settlers moved to St. Laurent in the aftermath of the Red River Rebellion ending in 1870. The broader area was formally incorporated as the Rural Municipality of St. Laurent in 1882. French-Canadians, Bretons, and Mennonites arrived in the first half of the 20th century.\nOak Point was the furthest northern corner of Manitoba when it was proclaimed a province in 1870. Due to its size and shape, it was nicknamed the \"Postage Stamp\" province.\nGet in.\nSt. Laurent is 1 hour northwest of Winnipeg on PTH 6.\nGet around.\nThe area is predominantly rural so a private car is a necessity.\nSee.\nSt. Laurent offers different events that celebrate its M\u00e9tis heritage.\nDo.\nThe following beaches are found along Lake Manitoba:"}
{"id": "183666", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183666", "title": "Olompali\u2013Petaluma\u2013Sonoma historic parks", "text": "California has several dozen state parks that focus on historic locations. Three of them just north of the San Francisco Bay have significant connections to the rancho era of Mexican Republic. This itinerary will take you through these three state historic parks in a day trip from the Bay area. Each is near a different city in California Wine Country.\nUnderstand.\nMost historic parks focus on California's early history, especially the many California indigenous people and the Spanish and Mexican settlers. These three show the people native to this area plus two sites associated with General Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo, a Californio (a California-born person of Spanish descent) who became a significant part of the Mexican government and eventually in the formation of the State of California.\nThe first park, Olompali, is part of the traditional territory of the Coast Miwok people, who lived in this area for thousands of years and in a village in what is now this park for about 1500 years. The Miwok hoipu (head man) built an adobe house here in 1776, and in 1843, his son was granted their land by the Mexican government under the rancho system.\nThe next stop is the Petaluma Abode. During the Mexican era, it was the largest privately owned building in what's now California. Once the headquarters for General Vallejo's Rancho Petaluma, it has been turned into a museum of ranch life at the time. Like many of the Californios, Vallejo lost most of his land and wealth in a series of court battles shortly after the California Gold Rush.\nThe last park is a collection of sites in the heart of Sonoma. On the western side, in a green park is Vallejo's Victorian-style wooden town home. This \"carpenter Gothic\" house was imported pre-built from the East Coast and assembled at this site in the 1850s. Next door is a building designed in Europe, which was called the Swiss Chalet. In the main downtown area are several other buildings from the era, including a mission church, a military barracks, a kitchen, and one of the oldest hotels in northern California.\nHours and prices.\nMost California state parks, especially outside urban areas, charge for parking but not for day use, which means that entrance may be free if you walk or bike into the park. Others have per-person entrance fees or separate fees for specific activities. The three parks listed in this itinerary are included in the California State Park's Historical Passport special day-use pass. As of 2021, this wallet card cost $50 and entitles the original owner to park one vehicle for free (if parking is charged inside the park) or to have up to four people enter the park (where entrance fees are charged per person) for a year.\nRelevantly for this itinerary, the ticket for day-use parking fees is accepted at almost all of the other state parks in the system for the same day. This means that if you pay the $8 to park at Olompali, you can get free parking at almost any other state park on the same day. The entrance fees at the other two parks in this itinerary are paid per person; however, the Petaluma and Sonoma sites have a sharing agreement, so if you pay at the first you visit ($3 per adult, $2 per child), you can get into the other for free.\nMany state parks are open from sunrise to sunset for people who want to walk around outdoors. The hours listed are the times when the main museum or other facilities are open.\nDogs.\nYou can bring dogs to most state parks. Normally, dogs must be kept on a leash and are not allowed in the buildings. In some parks, dogs are only allowed in certain areas. These restrictions are not always predictable, so it's best to check the details for each park separately. Overall, this itinerary isn't great for dogs.\nGet in.\nFrom San Francisco, take Highway 101 north across the Golden Gate Bridge. About 10 miles north of the bridge in Mill Valley, Highway 1, also called the Pacific Coast Highway, will fork off to the west to find the coast. Stay on Highway 101 for another 13 miles, to Novato. Take exit 463 toward San Marin Dr, and then turn left onto Atherton Ave to get to the western side of Highway 101. Then turn right onto Redwood Blvd, which is a service road that runs north along the highway. The main entrance to the first park is on the left.\nGet around.\nMost people would drive a car to these sites, but it's possible to visit all of these parks on bike or by bus. As you're planning, remember that traffic can be difficult. What should be a 30-minute drive may take more than an hour of creeping along a congested two-lane highway, especially on holidays or weekends.\nThese parks can be visited using the local bus systems if you don't mind walking for up to an hour after you get off the bus. If you decide to try this, consider using the Clipper card payment system, which will cost $3 for the card but get you a discount on the fares and obviate the need for carrying exact change. If you want to combine a bus ride with a bike trip, then all of the buses have a bike rack on the front can hold two or three bicycles (first come, first served). The nearest bus stops for the three parks are:\nSee.\nOur first stop is Olompali State Historic Park, just north of Novato. The area's history began with the people who were here before the missions crept north and before Mexico declared its independence from Spain. If you spend the morning at Olompali, then you can have lunch in Petaluma and dinner in Sonoma.\nWhen you leave Olompali, you'll be going south on Redwood Blvd. Turn left onto Atherton Ave to cross the highway, and then turn left to get back onto Highway 101 North towards Eureka. Nine miles later, take exit 472B onto Hwy 116 East, also called Lakeville Hwy. (If you're looking for lunch, you want to take exit 472A to Petaluma Blvd S. There are multiple restaurants on and near Petaluma Blvd S, about two or three miles off the highway.) Less than a mile after taking exit 472B off of Highway 101, turn left onto Casa Grande Rd. Follow Casa Grande Rd, straight through the traffic circle by the high school, until it dead-ends at Old Adobe Rd. You can see the old building through the trees from that intersection, but to find a place to park, you'll want to turn right onto Old Adobe Rd and then take the first left into the park's driveway.\nThe same Mexican general owned a house and managed a military outpost in the city of Sonoma, about 10 miles to the east, which is preserved as part of the Sonoma State Historic Park. To get there, turn left out of the park's entrance, which puts you back on Old Adobe Rd. Follow Old Adobe Rd southeast for 3.5 miles to the intersection with Hwy 116 E, also called Stage Gulch Rd, where the road will curve to the left. Follow that for 2.5 miles, past Carneros Vintners, and then turn left onto Watmaugh Rd. Watmaugh makes an easy 90\u00b0 turn to the right, and you'll still be on Watmaugh Rd. Drive another two miles on Watmaugh Rd, and then turn left onto Broadway (aka Hwy 12 West). This will bring you into town. Follow Broadway two miles north, until the street reaches Napa St. Broadway dead-ends here, facing Sonoma Plaza. The official entrance is on the other side of the tree-filled plaza, so turn right onto Napa St, then immediately left onto 1st St E, and start looking for an empty parking space on the street, or go another block, and find the public parking lot behind the Sonoma Barracks.\nIf you're ready for dinner, downtown Sonoma has many restaurants.\nTo get back to Highway 101, and thus to San Francisco and the rest of the Bay area, re-trace your path back to Petaluma. The total distance is about 13 miles. Find E Napa St by the Plaza, and turn south onto Broadway. Follow Broadway for two miles, and turn right on to Watmaugh Rd. Follow Watmaugh back through the curve (to the left, this time) onto the other section of Watmaugh Rd, and so forth, until you are less than half a mile from the old adobe. However, instead of turning right to go to the Petaluma Adobe State Historic Park and Casa Grande Rd again, keep going straight. The road will change names to Frates Rd, and it will lead you down to CA-116/Lakeview Hwy, which in turn will take you to Hwy 101."}
{"id": "183668", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183668", "title": "West Interlake", "text": "This is a destination-level article covering a large, sparsely populated rural area with multiple small towns and villages.\nThe Rural Municipality of West Interlake is located in the Interlake Region of Manitoba, Canada. A sparsely-populated area includes the communities of Ashern and Eriksdale.\nUnderstand.\nThe area serves as a transition area between the agricultural southern portion of Manitoba and its boreal forests\nAgriculture and its related businesses continue to be the major economic activity in the area. Cattle production is prominent with grain and hay being grown. The local fishing industry is very active during the winter months. Commercial fishing on Lake Manitoba remains a major source of income for some residents.\nBoth Ashern and Eriksdale serve a good pitstops for travelers on PTH 6 heading between Winnipeg and northern Manitoba.\nGet in.\nWest Interlake is about an hour and a half northwest of Winnipeg on PTH 6.\nGet around.\nAshern and Eriksdale are about 25 minutes by car from each other. The Narrows is 35 minutes west of PTH 6 on PTH 68.\nBuy.\nAshern is the regional service centre and has basic shopping. Eriksdale also has some basic services. "}
{"id": "183674", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183674", "title": "Buis-les-Baronnies", "text": "Buis-les-Baronnies is in the Ard\u00e8che and Dr\u00f4me region of France.\nUnderstand.\nBuis-les-Baronnies is in France, in the Dr\u00f4me and the region Auvergne Rh\u00f4ne-Alpes. This city was built in the 13th century and destroyed by the religious war in the 16th century.\nGet in.\nYou can go at Buis-les-Baronnies by car, by train at Avignon TGV station or with the bus line 39.\nGet around.\nThere are not lots of means of transport. There are only two; car and bus line 39.\nSee.\nYou can see mountains because Buis-les-Baronnies is in the mountains. Around Buis-les-Baronnies there are also lots of rivers and Lavender fields. It's beautiful. In this city, there are three churches, for example Saint Trophinne. Every Saturday morning, in the city center there is a big market with lots of variety and traditional products.\nDo.\nIn Buis-les-Baronnies you can do very fun sports activities like climbing, via ferrata, canyoning.\nBuy.\nThere are two supermarkets in BlB; U express and Carrefour Contact. In the centre of the town, you'll be able to buy some souvenir items, local items and clothing.\nEat and drink.\nIn this city, in the centre there are lots of restaurants and bars. The Ribote and the Grill du Four \u00e0 Pain have the top marks. These restaurants do also bar.\nSleep.\nBuis-les-Baronnies doesn't have hotels but you can book a house or a villa; there is silence. There is also a farmhouse Saint Julian's."}
{"id": "183675", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183675", "title": "Durfort", "text": "Durfort is in Tarn. It is a beautiful village to quote a Montagne Noir.\nUnderstand.\nDurfort is the place of boiler making around copper.\nGet in.\nYou can come only by car.\nGet around.\nYou can bike and walk in the streets. The streets are beautiful because the walls are in stone and are crossed by a river.\nSee.\nYou can see a tour of the city on the origin of the copper village a visits the Copper Museum.\nDo.\nRock climbing is available at via Ferrata de Malamort. You can rent equipment in \"Le Cyrano-Bar Resto'rapit\" (+33 06 79 13 95 73).\nBuy.\nAs glassmaking is a traditional craft in the area, you can visit \"L'atelier au verre[dead link]\" (a glassmaking workshop) that sells glassware. l' Atelier de Vannerie Gendre[dead link] is a traditional wicker-maker.\nSleep.\nYou can't sleep in Durfort but you can in Sor\u00e8ze."}
{"id": "183676", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183676", "title": "Pomerols", "text": "Pomerols is a town in the H\u00e9rault region of France.\nUnderstand.\nPomerols is a little city of France in H\u00e9rault (34), her surface area is of 11, 01 km2, and there is \u2248 2243 people in 2015.\nGet in.\nTo go in Pomerols, you can take the car, bus, train and also the plane. The nearest airport is the one of Cap d'Agde ().\nGet around.\nThen, you can ride with car, bike, bus and walk in the little city.\nSee.\nIn Pomerols there is a little museum on the history of the city and you can see also the Property of \"Grenati\u00e8re\" it's the landscapes of green spaces of Pomerols.\nDo.\nFor the activity, there is the beach, crossing the pond of Thau, discovery of sailing and also many other activities in family...\nBuy.\nTo buy memories, you can go at wine cellar of Pomerols (\u260f), it's a speciality of the city. And there is the delicatessen Valery Jean (\u260f), other first specialities of Pomerols.\nEat.\nFor the food, you can eat at \"Au Cowboy\" (\u260f) and \"Les Sens Ciel\" (\u260f) it's delicious restaurants of the Pomerols city.\nSleep.\nNex, to sleep you can go in guest room \"Chez Serge et Isa\" (\u260f) and property \"Fon de Rey\" (\u260f). There is also \"Villa les Olives\" in Florensac."}
{"id": "183677", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183677", "title": "Mirepoix/Tarn", "text": "Mirepoix-sur-Tarn is in Haute-Garonne\nUnderstand.\nMirepoix/Tarn is a small village in Haute-Garonne, Occitanie. It is a part of the community of villages \"Val'A\u00cfgo\". It is 27km away from Toulouse. Thanks to agriculture, it is a developing village. They build many habitations and try to be more attractive. On 18 November 2019, there was a dramatic event: a truck of 50 tons passed on the bridge and the bridge fell down. The maximum weight for the bridge was 19 tons. The man who drove the truck and a 15-year-old girl died in the accident.\nGet in.\nYou can take the busline Hop!304 from Balma-Gramont, where there is the subway station to Toulouse or the 355 bus line,from Toulouse station.\nGet around.\nThe village is really small so you only need your legs or a bike!\nSee.\nYou can see the Ste Juliette and St Cyr Church. Next to the graveyard you have a very old wash house. You can see the place and the city hall or the memorial.\nDo.\nIn Mirepoix/ Tarn, you can do many nature-based activities, like biking, walking and fishing. You can play petanque or tennis because there is a tennis court, and there is a motocross ground. In June, before the coronavirus, there was a festival of Comedia Dall'Arte, during three days, and at Christmas, there was a little spectacle and there was a celebration at the start of the year and a little funfair in summer. You can rent the function room for birthday or other celebration.\nBuy.\nBefore, there was a bakery (+ grocery and newsagent) but it caught fire so now you only have a bread deposit. But there are many pretty villages around with shops, like in Bessi\u00e8res: you have all sorts of shops and particularly the famous garden center Solignac. This is one of the biggest in Occitanie, it is open everyday. You can find plants, trees, animals, decoration, clothes, food and many other things. There is even a little farm! It is magnificent in Christmas. It is at around three minutes by car of Mirepoix, but now it is less convenient because of the fall of the bridge.\nEat.\nThere is a traditional French restaurant named \"La Grande Recr\u00e9e\". It is in an ancient school."}
{"id": "183678", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183678", "title": "Langeais", "text": "Langeais is a city on the Loire river in Centre-Val de Loire.\nUnderstand.\nTo the Castle of Langeais was rebuilt by Louis XI in 1465, it replaced a first fortified castle built at the end of the tenth century by Foulque Nerra.\nGet in.\nTo go at Langeais you can go by:\n- plane\n- car\n- SNCF train station\nGet around.\nTo move around the city it's possible to there:\n-by car\n-by bike\n-by walk\nSee.\nIn Langeais you can see the Castle of Langeais, it is in the city center and it's possible to visiting, the Langeais Bridge it is 358 meters long and also you can see the Saint-Baptiste Church\nDo.\nIf you won't bored, here are a few small things to do during your stays:\n- The Golfetvous, to play golf with family, friends or alone\n- Visiting the Grotte de la Roche-Cotard, paleolitic site\nBuy.\nTo buy memories from Langeais, you can buy in the Maison des vins. Lots of wines are kept there and are ready to be sold. Or La Savonni\u00e8re du Z\u00e8bre there are lots of soap with different scents.\nEat.\nHere is some ideas of places to eat:\n-La Blanche Hermine next to the castle with a good lunch and it's an excellent restaurant\n-Au coin des Halles at 100 meters from the castle, it's a very good restaurant\n-Caf\u00e9 du Chateau, simple and good, with a brawery with good price for the quality\nSleep.\nTo sleep you can go in:\n-La Fleur du Lys at 200 meters from the castle from \n-L'ange est r\u00eaveur with garden, terrace and free wifi from \n-Le Clos Marie with swimming pool, garden and terrace from "}
{"id": "183679", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183679", "title": "Limoux", "text": "Limoux is in Aude in the south of France. The town has been known since the 16th century for the production of a renowned sparkling wine, blanquette de Limoux. This sparkling wine from Languedoc, which plays a major part in the city's economy, benefits from a protected designation of origin (PDO) classification. The city is also the site of a carnival which lasts several months.\nUnderstand.\nLimoux is a little town of more than 10,000 inhabitants (2021). You will find a lot of things to do. It's a very cute town with its history.\nGet in.\nTo get to Limoux, you can take the train because there is the Flassian train station or the bus. But you can go by car too, of course, or bicycle.\nDrink.\nYou can't go to Limoux without tasting one of the wines of Limoux or the iconic sparkling wine (\"blanquette\"), that you can find in the shop \"Sieur d'Arques\".\nSleep.\nIf you search for a place where you can sleep, the \"Logis Modern et pigeon\" is here. It's the most chic hotel in Limoux, it has a \"Art-Deco\" famous and a gastronomic restaurant (04 68 31 00 25). There are not a lot of hotels in Limoux; what you will find especially are the guest rooms or apartments like \"Lovely Limoux\"."}
{"id": "183684", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183684", "title": "Olargues", "text": "Olargues is a town in the H\u00e9rault region of France.\nUnderstand.\nIn the beginning of XVII century, Olargues was successively occupied by the Romans, the Vandals and the Visigoths.\nIn 1629, the \"Citadelle\" was destroyed by \"Cardinal de Richelieu\" and there was \"the war of Religions\" too.\nDuring this century, \"Saint-Laurent church\" was built in fact you can find some old bulwark of the village like \"la porte Neuve\", \"la porte Gaubert\" and \"la porte des Pans\".\nSee.\nIn this village you can see a lot of beautiful things, like the \"Mus\u00e9e d'arts et de traditions\", discover \"l'aigle royal\" and the different type of birds or \"Le Mouflon\" on \"Haut-Languedoc\".\nFurthermore, you can observe the interior design medieval and \"La Roche du Pitou\".\nSleep.\nAfter getting in the village you can rest yourself in hotels with 4 stars, for example \"Les Quatre Farceurs\" or \"L'H\u00f4tel des chats noirs\".\nThose hotels are comfortable and have a good service, the price is 84$ per night which is not really much."}
{"id": "183685", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183685", "title": "Saint-Mont", "text": "Saint-Mont is a town in Gers.\nGet in.\nTo go in Saint-Mont, you can take the car, the bus or the plane because the airport of Toulouse is at 151km of Saint-Mont.\nGet around.\nTo move in Saint-Mont, you can use the car like mean of motorized transport. But, you can also use the bike or walk but it's energetic because the floor of Saint-Mont is not flat.\nSee.\nIn Saint-Mont, you can see the church of Jean Baptiste, this monument is classified like a historical monument in France. Moreover, you can see the monastery of Saint-Mont of Saint-Mont which was a strategic place for the Romain and for the French revolution after.And, you can also see the river of L'Adour which joins the river of the Garonne.\nDo.\nYou can do a lot of activities in Saint-Mont, you can do wine tasting, you can do hiking in the village, in the forest inside Saint-Mont, or in L'Adour. In this place, you can also do biking. And, you can do canoe kayak in L'Adour.\nEat.\nIf you want to eat, you can go in Les Ber\u00eats Noirs which proposes typical aliments of Saint-Mont. You can also goin the monastery of Saint-Mont which also does restaurant with specialties of Saint-Mont.\nDrink.\nIf you want to drink, you can drink in La Cave: the wine of Saint-Mont is famous. \nSleep.\nTo sleep in Saint-Mont, there is one option: the monastery of Saint-Mont. It's a historical monument but also a hotel with catering, pool, restaurant, sauna...You can take an option with full option or half board. The price starts with the night. (06 32 86 46 11)"}
{"id": "183686", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183686", "title": "Bidart", "text": "Bidart is a town in Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es-Atlantiques.\nUnderstand.\nLocated between Biarritz and Saint Jean de Luz, Bidart is a lovely place of the Basque coast, quiet and close to the big cities. The name Bidart comes from \"Bide Artean\" which means \"at the crossroads\". Indeed, various roads from Biarritz, Bayonne and many other cities end in Bidart.\nGet in.\nYou can arrive at the Biarritz airport or Biarritz station and then take a taxi or hire a car. There are also many bus lines from Biarritz to Bidart.\nGet around.\nIn Bidart, you can get around by bus or by car. There are many bike lanes, so riding a bike is convenient. Of course you can just walk to visit the village.\nSee.\nIn Bidart, there is the \"Chapelle Sainte Madeleine\", at a viewing point. From this point, you can even go down to a beach by a small path. There is also the village square, with the huge Basque pelota wall!\nDo.\nIn this village, you can practice a huge number of activities, from traditional Basque pelota to trekking along the coastal path. Or else, obviously, just relax on one of the six beaches. You can also take surfing or horse riding lessons. There is a water park with a lot of attraction called \"Bid'a Parc\".\nBuy.\nIf you go to the Basque country, you have to taste at least once the traditional Basque cake, with cream or black cherry, depending on what you prefer. You can also bring back a beach towel, or any other object with a red Basque cross.\nEat.\nYou can go to the old Bassilour mill, in which handmade Basque cakes are sold. In front of this mill, there is the \"Restaurant du Moulin\" with an amazing terrace surrounded by trees. It's a great atmosphere and prices are affordable. If you prefer the beach, you can go to the \"Bela Gorri\". This restaurant is divided into two parts: The restaurant, at the seaside, very cosy, decorated in red tones with a dim light. It has a direct access to the beach, and it's very convenient for children. And before going to eat a good meal, you can have an aperitif and relax at the terrace bar that completes the restaurant. Comfortably sat on large coloured cushions to admire the sunset. Sometimes, there are small concerts ! \nSleep.\nIn Bidart, there are many campsites and hotels. For example, the \"Hotel Elissada\", on the village's central area, so it's very convenient. There is also the \"Domaine de Bassilour\" for a romantic setting, away from the center. It's a calm and quiet place, perfect for relaxing with a very warm atmosphere. One night costs 124 \u20ac. Or you can go to \"Hotel Gochoki\", if you want a cheaper hotel (86 \u20ac/ per night). This hotel has a beautiful park in which you can take your breakfast, with local and organic products. It also has a heated pool and it is close to the center. "}
{"id": "183687", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183687", "title": "Boutx", "text": "Boutx is a town in the Haute-Garonne region of Occitanie, France.\nUnderstand.\nBoutx is a small village of 240 inhabitants situated in France, in Occitanie, in the departement of the \"Haute-Garonne\" in the mountains named the Pyrenees. The postcode is 31440 and the nearby village is: \"Saint-B\u00e9at-Lez\". The village is not high as you can think, it is in a low part of these mountains. In the past, the village was named \"Bouts\", it is an old gallo-roman village. The inhabitants are the \"Boutxois\" and the \"Boutxoises\".\nGet in.\nBy car.\nTo go to Boutx, car is your only option, there aren't any airports or train stations because it is in the Pyrenees so, the village is in a mountain pass. To come to Boutx by car, take the D44 road.\nGet around.\nTo visit or get around the village in all its places, there are no buses. The only way for you to discover is by car, by bike or while walking.\nSee.\nIn the closest village \"Saint-B\u00e9at-Lez\", there is the \"G\u00e9ry\" lake, it is a lovely place with a good view, a lot of nature and trees.\nDo.\nBoutx has a lot of visitors because of its ski resort. It is named \"Le Mourtis\", this ski resort is a family and friendly place, the majority are easy blue and red ski-runs. It is small and there are not many people in this ski resort because it is not high: only 1860 meters above sea level so, you will find only families or friend groups.\nMoreover, \"Le Mourtis\" is not expensive, the ski pass is around \u20ac230 for 2 adults and 2 children. In Boutx, if you aren't here to ski, you can do cycling in the \"Mont\u00e9\" pass or some hiking and mountain trails on the peaks of \"Escales\", \"Escalettes\" and \"Pi\u00e9jau\".\nBuy.\nTo purchase your mountain stuff, there are 5 equipment rental shops of skis, snowboards, sledges and snowshoes. These shops are named \"L'Isba\", \"l'Ousset Sport\", \"Le Y\u00e9ti\", \"Le Tuc de L'Etang\" and the \"Pyrenees ski\". \"L'Ousset Sport\" is the closest one of \"Le Mourtis\". \nTo buy your food you have one grocery shop which is the \"Vival\".\nEat.\nTo eat after a good day of skiing, you will have the choice. In the snack-bars named \"Chez Marie\", \"Le Louzat\", L'Igloo\" and \"La Grange\" which are at the foot of the ski-runs. Go here if you want some waffles, crepes and hot chocolates in the end of the afternoon at 18p.m. For information, \"L'Igloo\" is a pub open from 8a.m to 10p.m. The restaurant \"La Grange\" is also a gourmet restaurant the evening and only by reservation.\nSleep.\nTo sleep, you have the choice in Boutx, you can rent some traditional chalets made in old wood from a private landlord or not. For example, you have the chalet of the \"Bazet\" where can sleep 5 to 7 people. You will find also \u00e9in this tiny village some hostels like the one named \"Les oursons\", which has free Wi-Fi, full-board available and 8 rooms from 2 to 6 people. It is situated at 160 meters of the ski resort of Boutx.\nConnect.\nAs of Aug 2023, Boutx has 4G from all French carriers, with a patchy signal on the approach roads. 5G has not yet reached town."}
{"id": "183688", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183688", "title": "Pechbonnieu", "text": "Pechbonnieu is a commune in Haute-Garonne.\nUnderstand.\nPechbonnieu was born by the merge of 2 different villages (Cstillon and la Basstide de-Constance). Pechbonnieu was created in the 20th century.\nGet in.\nTo come to France you can take the plane because there is an airport in Toulouse. Otherwise, you can take the boat and go to Bordeaux. To come in Pechbonnieu there are 2 means of transport: the car, and the bus.\nGet around.\nTo visit Pechbonnieu there are different means of transport: \nSee.\nPechbonnieu has historic elements, for example there is the vestige of an old castle. This castle was built in the 18e century.\nOther than that there is the \"church\". The \"Church\" is an old annex of the parish of Montberon. The \"Church\" was built in the 18th century.\nEat.\nIn Pechbonnieu there are many types of restaurants. For example there is \"Original sushi.\" This restaurant is vegetarian and there are many specialities of Japan.\nTo eat pizza there are \"A fleur de Pizz' \" and \"B'oogie's Pizza \". The price of each pizza is around 12 \u20ac.\nAnd to finish \"L'esquisse\" is a brewery with many good recipes.\nSleep.\nTo sleep in Pechbonnieu there are different places, for example, there is \"G\u00eete de la Roche\", a guest house. In this guesthouse there is a beautiful landscape, it's calm and it's convivial. The price for only 1 night is \u20ac40.\nOr there is \"Aux terrasses de kaya\". This place is calm (without many houses around), they make very good breakfast and from the bed one can see the Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9e. The price is \u20ac70/night\nConnect.\nAs of Aug 2023, Pechbonnieu and its approach roads have 4G from Bouygues and SFR, and 5G from Free and Orange. \nGo next.\nOnward routes by public transport involve backtracking via Toulouse."}
{"id": "183689", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183689", "title": "Voiron", "text": "Voiron is a city in Is\u00e8re, France. The city is at the foot of the Chartreuse, a mountain chain in Alps, so that is convenient for winter sports.\nUnderstand.\nVoiron is a small city of 20,000 inhabitants (2018) in Is\u00e8re, a French department. Voiron was famous in the 19th century, for its stationery and textile industry; Today, Voiron has a lot of very famous brands, like Rossignol, a winter sports brand, or Ant\u00e9site sirups, very famous in France. The city is also famous for the Monast\u00e8re des P\u00e8res Chartreux, an old monastery always active composed of monks who make a famous liquor, the Chartreuse, and a few other trademarks.\nGet in.\nYou can arrive in Voiron in plane, to Grenoble Airport, by train, bus, car, bicycle, motorbike, walk etc\nSee.\nA very great activity in this city is the visit of the Caves de Chartreus. Located in the centre of the city, you can visit the old caves of the production of Chartreuse liquors. There also is a final degustations of all types of Chartreuse liquor, like the Chartreuse Verte or the Chartreuse Jaune, so it is worth it! This old caves were originally the official production site of Chartreuse liquors, but it was moved in 2014 to another place, more secure and outside the city, because the enormous amount of alcohol can explode.\nDo.\nThere is a lot of activities in Voiron, like the climb of the Vouise, a small mountain next to the city, and, at the top, the statue of Notre-Dame de la Vouise.\nThere also is the theater of the Grand-Angle, PassrL cinemas, a minigolf course, a Laser Game and, for the readers, a media library.\nBuy.\nThere is a few places in Voiron where you can spend your money.\nThere is a local chocolate shop: La Chocolaterie Bonnat. The familial chocolatery is making chocolate and others sweets since the 19th century. Their chocolate is extremely great, the shop is enormous and it participate at the economy and the culture of Voiron.\nThere are also a few textile shops and a few libraries."}
{"id": "183691", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183691", "title": "Saint-Antonin-Noble-Val", "text": "Saint-Antonin-Noble-Val is a picturesque medieval village of 2,800 people (2018) in the Tarn-et-Garonne region of France. It has a preserved largely medieval core which has many listed buildings, and is a major tourist attraction. Its Sunday market is extensive and draws many visitors and locals.\nUnderstand.\nThe town was used as a location for the 2001 film \"Charlotte Gray\", starring Cate Blanchett.\nIt was used as a location in the film \"The Hundred-Foot Journey\" starring Helen Mirren released in August 2014.\nHistory.\nThe area has been occupied for over 10,000 years, as evidenced by the archaeological discoveries at the nearby Upper Palaeolithic (Magdalenian) site of Fontal\u00e8s.\nThe town is named after Antoninus of Pamiers, who brought Christianity to the Rouergue. He was martyred c. 305 when, following this success, he then tried to convert Pamiers, his home town in the Pyrenees. \nGet in.\nYou can go to Saint-Antonin-Noble-Val with a bus, a train, a car or a taxi.\nIf you can start in Toulouse, the price of a travel with a bus is \u20ac2 but with taxi and train is \u20ac45-70.\nGet around.\nYou can visit Saint-Antonin-Noble-Val with a bike and a canoe and by foot of course!\nSee.\nSeveral houses are designated as \"Monuments historiques\":\nEat and drink.\nThe most famous restaurant in the city is \"La Fringale Chez Fred\". All products of this restaurant are local products. On the menu there are more than 15 beers.\nSleep.\nIn Saint-Antonin-Noble-Val, you can sleep in many hotels, campings and guest houses, like \"G\u00eete Chez St\u00e9phanie et J\u00e9romme\". This house costs \u20ac55 for one night (2021). The house is equipped with a big pool and a beautiful view. Around house there are many hiking paths. \nIn the city there is a camping \"le noble-Val\". In this camping there is a pool a library a different fields of football, p\u00e9tanque and volley. The camping is a 15-min walk to go to the village."}
{"id": "183693", "revid": "1342202", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183693", "title": "Leucate", "text": "Leucate is a Mediterranean coastal town in the Aude region of Occitanie, southern France.\nUnderstand.\nLeucate is a village not far off Perpignan and Narbonne and with a beach on the M\u00e9diterranean sea. Leucate comes from the Greek, Leukos, which means white, because in the old city, the castle and houses were built with white rock\nGet in.\nTo go to Leucate, you can take the car if you are not very far. If you come from the North of France for example, you can take the train, stop you in Perpignan and take the car to arrive to Leucate. But if you come from another country, you need to take airplane to Carcassone and take a car after.\nGet around.\nOnce in Leucate, you can take a bike because it's a little city and you can visit all the city on bike, if you have motivation, you can stay in the village and do what you want on foot, but if you want to visit village or city a little bit far from Leucate, you need a car.\nSee.\nIf you go to Leucate, you can take your car and go to visit the African Reserve of Sigean, there a lot of African animals like lion, elephant... it's a great experience especially for kids and family, it's 20 min from Leucate.\nIf you want an activity near more, between the 28 April and 2 may, there are \"Le mondial du vent\" in La Franqui. It's a big event where you can see the world cup of kitesurf for example.\nIf you like hiking, you can visit the ruin of the Leucate Castle in the top of the cliff.\nDo.\nIn Leucate and not far of it, there are a lot of things to do. For example, you can try extreme sports like kitesurf, windsurf or sand yacht. If you prefer, you can go to the beach which is absolutely beautiful.\nBuy.\nWhen you come back to your home, don't forget to buy a wine bottle of Leucate, it is excellent, and a box of oyster which is the speciality of the region.\nEat and Drink.\nIn Leucate, there are a lot of restaurants and bars. You can go to \"La Bodega el Flamingo\" to taste tapas and regional products. It's open from 7:00 pm to 0:00 am and it closes Monday, the address is 115 avenue Jean Jaures. If you prefer the French kitchen, you can go to \"Le Jardin de Filoche\". It's open from 9:00 am just Mondays, the address is 4 avenue Jean Jaures. But if you prefer simple food, you can go to \"Pizza Portofino\", open from 6:00 pm all days except Monday. The address is 153 avenue Charles de Gaulle. And for groups between 18 and 25 years, you can go to \"Biquet\"; It's a bar open all day and it is on the beach, it's so funny if you go with your friends.\nSleep.\nIf you go to Leucate, you need a place to sleep. So you can go to the hotel \"Le Leukos\", it has 4.1 stars, the price of a room is between \u20ac77 and \u20ac101 for one night, it is at 151 Avenue Charles De Gaulle, and this hotel is 1\u00e0 min walk from the beach. Or if you prefer, you can sleep in the \"19-21 un amour d'hotel\"\". It is noted 4,8 stars, this hotel has a bar, a swimming pool on the top and you can ask for a massage in your bedroom. The price of a room is \u20ac204 for a night and it is on the 19 avenue Francus Vals. It is in the city center."}
{"id": "183701", "revid": "2229778", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183701", "title": "Horsham (Victoria)", "text": "Horsham is a town in the western region of Victoria along the Western Highway.\nUnderstand.\nHorsham is a town located by the Western Highway, a popular freight link and a national highway. It's a town that is popular with stopovers.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nHorsham can be accessed from the A8 Western Highway within 4 hours drive from Melbourne."}
{"id": "183707", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183707", "title": "Bundelkhand", "text": "Bundelkhand (Hindi: \u092c\u0941\u0902\u0926\u0947\u0932\u0916\u0902\u0921 \"Bundelkha\u1e47\u1e0d\") is a geographical and cultural region of India, divided between the states of Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh. See:\nNorthern Madhya Pradesh is sometimes considered part of Bundelkhand as well."}
{"id": "183708", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183708", "title": "Carmaux", "text": "Carmaux is in Occitanie.\nUnderstand.\nCarmaux is located north of Albi. It was once the city of coal, whose presence still marks the place and its surroundings, even if some mines have been transformed into leisure centers.\nSee.\nYou can visit the Cit\u00e9 Mini\u00e8re of Carmaux if you like history and learning about a city. If you love art and glass, you can visit the Mus\u00e9e de verre of Carmaux. But if you love sports, it is possible for you to visit Cap\u2019Decouvert. It is a large park with a lot of sports activities and it is very funny and cool.\nEat.\nThere are a lot of restaurants with low prices in Carmaux like these restaurants, \u00d4 Saveurs d\u2019Antan, its gastronomic French food, Le Boeuf Marin, it's a mid gastronomic restaurant. Or if you like simpler food, you can eat in L\u2019Eclat.&lt;mapframe latitude=\"44.049473\" longitude=\"2.158127\" zoom=\"12\" width=\"400\" height=\"363\" align=\"right\"&gt;\n \"type\": \"FeatureCollection\",\n \"features\": [\n \"type\": \"Feature\",\n \"properties\": {},\n \"geometry\": {\n \"type\": \"Polygon\",\n \"coordinates\": [\n [\n 2.1354675292968754,\n 44.039725114583696\n [\n 2.1354675292968754,\n 44.059712566971974\n [\n 2.181816101074219,\n 44.059712566971974\n [\n 2.181816101074219,\n 44.039725114583696\n [\n 2.1354675292968754,\n 44.039725114583696\n]\n]\n&lt;/mapframe&gt;\nSleep.\nIn Carmaux, you can sleep in many places like the G\u00eete \u00abDe La Fontaine Verte\u00bb or G\u00eete \u00abQuartier de la tour\u00bb.\nThere is also a hotel called \u00abLe Gambetta\u00bb, and Carmaux has a good reputation for camping sites."}
{"id": "183710", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183710", "title": "Abramtsevo", "text": "Abramtsevo (Russian: \u0410\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0301\u043c\u0446\u0435\u0432\u043e) is an estate located north of Moscow, in the proximity of Khotkovo, that became a center for the Slavophile movement and artistic activity in the 19th century. The estate is located in the village of the same name, in Sergiyevo-Posadsky District of Moscow Oblast, and it is surrounded by beautiful mixed forests, in which spruce, oak and birch trees abound. The area also has numerous streams fed by spring waters.\nUnderstand.\nOriginally owned by author Sergei Aksakov, other writers and artists \u2014 such as Nikolai Gogol \u2014 at first came there as his guests. Under Aksakov, visitors to the estate discussed ways of ridding Russian art of Western influences to revive a purely national style. In 1870, eleven years after Aksakov's death, it was purchased by Savva Mamontov, a wealthy industrialist and patron of the arts.\nUnder Mamontov, Russian themes and folk art flourished there. During the 1870s and 1880s, Abramtsevo hosted a colony of artists who sought to recapture the quality and spirit of medieval Russian art in the manner parallel to the Arts and Crafts movement in Great Britain. Several workshops were set up there to produce handmade furniture, ceramic tiles, and silks imbued with traditional Russian imagery and themes.\nWorking together in a cooperative spirit, the artists Vasily Polenov and Viktor Vasnetsov designed a plain but picturesque church, with murals painted by Polenov, Vasnetsov and his brother, a gilded iconostasis by Ilya Repin and Mikhail Nesterov, and folklore-inspired sculptures by Viktor Hartmann and Mark Antokolsky. Towards the turn of the 20th century, drama and opera on Russian folklore themes (e.g., Rimsky-Korsakov's \"The Snow Maiden\") were produced in Abramtsevo by the likes of Konstantin Stanislavsky, with sets contributed by Vasnetsov, Mikhail Vrubel, and other distinguished artists.\nAbramtsevo is now open to the public and tourists can wander along the many paths through the surrounding forest and cross the wooden bridges that served as an inspiration for the artists at the Abramtsevo Colony. They can also visit many of the buildings to see works produced by the artists at the colony, e.g., a wooden bathhouse in the shape of a traditional dwelling of Ancient Rus, designed by Ivan Ropet, and the House on Chicken Legs, a fairy-tale abode of an evil witch, Baba Yaga, designed by Vasnetsov. One building, the main \"manor,\" is said to have been the model for the estate in which Anton Chekhov set The Cherry Orchard."}
{"id": "183712", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183712", "title": "Thuir", "text": "Thuir is in Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es-Orientales.\nUnderstand.\nThuir is a little Catalan town located near Perpignan. Thuir took a place in the French-Spanish war.\nGet in.\nTo go to Thuir, you can take trains or take the planes. Catch a flight on Perpignan's Airline, and then continue driving in a car until you reach Thuir.\nSee.\nIn Thuir there are a lot of remains of fortifications and a museum of arts and traditions that you can go and see. There is also the Byrrh's cellars that you can visit and finally, you can see the largest barrel in the world and visit the production of the Byrrh's alcohol.\nDo.\nIf you travel to Thuir in August, there is the festival of the lawn the first week-end of August.\nYou can do carriage rides. \nClose to Thuir, away to Ponteilla there is a garden escape game and a botanical garden where you can go in family.\nEat.\nAt the Hotel Cortie there is a restaurant which makes the traditional rooster in the Byhrr according to recipe handed down from generation to generation\nConnect.\nAs of Jan 2024, Thuir and its approach highways have 5G from all French carriers."}
{"id": "183713", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=183713", "title": "Montgaillard", "text": "Montgaillard is in Tarn.\nUnderstand.\nMontgaillard is a little village based in Tarn, Occitanie, France. There is not a lot of inhabitants (383 in 2018) and it is a quite calm and quiet place.\nGet in.\nThe fastest route available to come to Montgaillard is definitely the D999. And if you prefer travelling by bus, there is the liO buses travelling all around the area.\nGet around.\nUnfortunately, the only way to travel across the village is by walking but by doing this you can visit in a much more detailed way.\nSee.\nThe local church of the village is quite beautiful, built in the neo-gothic style. There also is, hidden in the woods, a great castle that you can pass by when you are hiking.\nDo.\nThere is a lot of hiking trails starting, ending or just passing by Montgaillard. Try them and venture in the wild woods, across the medieval landscape and around the dark lakes. If you are lucky you will even have the chance of encountering a sneaky fox or a beautiful deer.\nBuy.\nThere is a bunch of local traders and you can meet them at the \"Salle des f\u00eates de Montgaillard\" where there is a local market every Sunday. The most popular product is the honey corporation called \"Ruchers de Boh\u00e8me\" and they make fresh and ecological honey for low prices.\nEat and drink.\n\"Le Bienvenu\" is a pub and a grocery store where you can buy local products or drink coffee. When comes the night, come watch a rugby match while eating some good food, there is always new people to meet.\nSleep.\nAfter your long days, you will have to sleep and \"Le Clos des Galichons\" is a perfect place with its comfy beds and clean chambers. And when you wake up, don't worry about the food your host will cook you an amazing breakfast for you and your family.\nAddress: 976 LA Mercaderie, 81630 Montgaillard\nPhone number: 05 63 34 68 25\nGo next.\nYou would like to visit Tauriac or Beauvais-sur-Tescou if you have enjoyed your visit to Montgaillard."}
